WEBVTT

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:10.770
For 160 million years, the dinosaurs ruled this world,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.920
while living in their shadows was a group of animals

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.900
which couldn't have been more different.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.060
These were our ancestors, small, furry creatures called mammals,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.000
clinging to safety wherever they could.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.910
The mammal's time would come.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:39.090
65 million years ago, volcanic activity started to poison the atmosphere.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:45.330
The last dinosaurs were already living on a sick planet

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.590
when their nemesis arrived from space.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.100
A meteor 10 kilometers wide slammed into Earth

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.940
to mark the end of the reign of dinosaurs.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:24.880
series is about what happened next. The survivors of the extinction all had one

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.700
thing in common, their size. Nearly every animal over 10 kilograms had been wiped

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:29.920
out.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.240
leaving a world of little creatures.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.360
Among them were the mammals.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:39.850
In Walking with Beasts, you will witness how mammals left behind these small beginnings

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.160
and took over the world.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.850
In the course of 20 million years, mammals got more and more successful

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:57.620
until they were the biggest, fiercest, and most spectacular animals on the planet.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Whatever the climate, whatever the habitat, mammals made it their own.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.620
Their great strength was their ability to adapt.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.090
They grew to gigantic sizes.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:17.680
They evolved into powerful killers, like the famous saber-toothed cats.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.270
And they even laid claim to the oceans.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.280
Then, around four million years ago, came mankind's own origins.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.980
In a type of ape that came...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.440
down from the trees and walked upright.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.600
Our story of this epic time finishes just 30,000 years ago

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.620
with the ice ages when our planet turned cold

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.220
and our ancestors hunted in the realm of the mammoth.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.800
It is a time called the Eocene.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.660
healed itself from the ravages of the massive meteor strike. Much has changed since the

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:41.350
time of the dinosaurs. It is far hotter now and lush tropical rainforest has sprung up

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:48.580
on every continent from the Arctic to Antarctica. In this flowering New Eden, meteors no longer

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:49.940
present as much of a threat.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.980
Under the forest canopy thrives a menagerie of weird and wonderful creatures.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.180
Here, the rule of scaly reptiles is a distant memory.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:13.460
The animals that dominate now are covered in feathers or fur.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:18.280
Mammals have adapted well to the new world and there is a staggering variety of them,

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:23.900
but they are still small and, as before, live under the shadow of bigger, deadlier animals.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.480
The dinosaurs might be long gone, but they left the world a vicious legacy.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.730
Their direct descendants are the birds.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:24.380
For the first and only time in its history, birds rule the earth.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.940
story of just 24 hours in one part of the Eocene's mysterious global forest.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.140
in an area that will one day become Germany.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.030
It is dawn, and at the base of a fig tree,

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:10.680
one animal has already had its first brush with death.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:20.600
This is a female Leptictidium.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.140
She is a metre long and a common sight in the forests of 50 million years ago.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:29.840
Her kind have survived virtually unchanged since the time of the dinosaur.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.850
life though is scarcely any easier now this is still a perilous world where she

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:43.400
must live fast and will probably die young typical mammals she looks after

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:47.470
her offspring until they are old enough to fend for themselves but with so many

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:54.740
predators about today is a bad day for her litter to leave the nest she however

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:59.160
has to feed whatever the risk she only has a short window of opportunity to do

00:06:59.160 --> 00:06:59.420
so

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.740
and that window is fading fast.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.670
The cool early morning is an ideal time

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:10.650
to catch the frogs, lizards, and insects she feeds on.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:14.960
Being cold-blooded, they have yet to warm up

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:16.370
and are still sluggish.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.460
She, however, is warm-blooded and fast-moving

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:20.680
24 hours a day.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.300
This is one of the mammal's ancient advantages.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.340
Also, to help track down her prey, she has an incredibly acute sense of hearing

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:40.220
and a distinctive, super-sensitive nose that can twitch to locate food among the leaf litter.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:52.520
She is agile enough to catch even flying insects.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:07.250
The best morning hunting lasts only an hour or two, and time is ticking.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.760
She must keep moving to find more.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:17.480
This time in Europe's history, Germany is at the center of much volcanic activity,

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:21.370
and this whole patch of forest is riddled with geothermal springs.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.600
In places, these form seething mud pools.

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:38.370
Boiling water, poisonous gas, and earthquakes are ever-present threats that the animals have learned to live with.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:42.110
But not every hazard here is so easy to ignore.

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:50.240
As the sun gets higher, the daylight creatures are becoming active, and that brings out the forest's more menacing side.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:58.380
The birds. They are the top predators in this weird forest.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.330
This is the largest, Gastornis, a half-ton pile of muscle and feathers, as tall as a grown man.

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:16.880
This is a female, and for the last two months her whole life has revolved around the one egg in her nest,

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:19.200
now only hours from hatching.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:29.150
She is fiercely territorial, and when another Gastornis gets too close, she moves to protect her nest.

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:35.160
Since the Great Extinction, birds have been a success like mammals.

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:37.450
But what is more, they have got big.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:41.190
Big enough to take over the role of the predatory dinosaurs.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:54.360
Two giants clash, the small mammal escapes.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:58.310
There are echoes here of a bygone age.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.720
The leptictirium needs a lot of food for her size.

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:26.130
Like all mammals, this is the price she pays for a warm-blooded metabolism.

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:29.910
There is usually plenty of prey down by the lakes.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:40.510
sight but this morning there is only trouble there is a newcomer down by the

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:52.880
lake stirring in the morning's early rays is an ambulocetus a bizarre beast

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:55.180
that has swum up the river from the nearby coast

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:01.120
He is a predator.

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:08.880
Her hunting is interrupted once again.

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.350
She must move on, and quickly.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:22.780
The three-meter-long carnivore waddles awkwardly towards the lake.

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:25.650
Although his ancestors hunted on land,

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:29.460
Ambulocetus has evolved to be far more at home in the water.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:38.860
In fact, his descendants will take this to a greater extreme.

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:42.340
You are looking at the very earliest form of whale.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:46.340
Ambulocetus, in fact, means walking whale.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:54.160
With another 10 million years of evolution, the limbs will become flippers and the tail will become a fluke.

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:57.910
His style of swimming already has the look of a whale or a dolphin.

00:11:58.540 --> 00:11:59.880
His body moves up and down.

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:04.250
and not side to side like the fishes or crocodiles he shares the water with.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:13.840
He is the most powerful predator in this lake, but he is far from safe here.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:16.550
There is a hidden peril.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.640
Huge quantities of volcanic gas are trapped in the lake bed.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:28.670
If enough of this gas were to escape at one time, it would suffocate the animals for miles around.

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:43.020
The lake is a time bomb, but the Ambulocetus thinks he has found the ideal spot for practicing his deadly speciality.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:48.720
Ambush.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:59.980
Propaleatherium, an early form of horse, presents the day's first...

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:29.880
good chance of a kill. This time the Ambulocetus fails, but the days long

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:32.540
and he will try again and again.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:42.770
For the Leptictidium, the forest is now too dangerous,

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:44.800
and the time for hunting is over.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:47.900
The mother returns to the safety of her nest.

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:50.220
She has not done well this morning,

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:53.560
and it is vital her next hunting trip is more successful.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:57.620
She will sleep now until the evening,

00:13:58.060 --> 00:13:59.320
when hunting will be easier.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.660
and the low light will help her evade the sharp-eyed birds.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:15.020
Noon, temperatures climb above 30 degrees.

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:18.150
The only movement is the Gastornis chick,

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:20.260
starting to break free from the egg.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:26.460
Not far away, the Leptictitium family

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:29.110
are still in their nest, but in their sleep,

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:29.940
they are-

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.020
totally defenseless against one particular predator.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:51.490
Giant, carnivorous ants.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.880
This is the largest species of ant ever.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:05.280
They are on the lookout for prey, any creature that can't get away in time.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:16.560
This is the vanguard of an ominous killing machine.

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:20.090
Behind them is an army of half a million others,

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:23.710
marching through the forest, stripping its prey to the burn.

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:29.970
Ants.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.200
have the next victim.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:25.000
Mid-afternoon, and the propaleatherium are busy foraging for grapes from the forest floor.

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:29.250
By modern standards, these primitive horses are tiny.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.080
But this is how horses started out, small, forest-dwelling animals.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:39.180
At this stage, they're not much bigger than cats and have yet to even develop hooves,

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:42.220
having instead four hoof-like toes.

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:53.180
In a forest where every tree could hide a predator, the horses live on a knife edge,

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:55.620
ready to spring at the slightest sound.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:00.290
Thank you.

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:38.490
As the light drops before sunset, the leptictidium stirs from her nest.

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:40.360
It's time to hunt again.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:49.120
But first, she must clear away escape paths around the tree.

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:53.550
This will give her a slightly better chance of outrunning any predators she meets.

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:55.750
And every little bit counts.

00:17:59.220 --> 00:17:59.980
Her young are eager to hunt.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.670
to join her this evening. Until now she has managed to keep them safe from the dangers of

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:10.240
the forest by suckling them in the nest. But there comes a time for all mammals when they can

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:29.310
no longer rely on their mother's milk. The young must now learn to find food for themselves and

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:29.880
there only

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.800
hope is to follow their mother's example. These are important lessons. By smelling the

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:44.310
insect she has caught, they can recognize what is safe to eat. However, their chances of

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:57.600
survival are slim. Most won't make it to adulthood. As dusk approaches, the predators are still

00:18:57.600 --> 00:18:58.340
out in numbers.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.080
Cymbulocetus has yet to make a kill today and is preparing another ambush near the shore of the lake.

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:12.460
Although he has no ears, he listens for approaching prey by putting his jaw to the ground and detecting vibrations.

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:15.900
It is the same mechanism that allows him to hear underwater.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:22.600
If any animal strays too close, he'll be waiting.

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.690
Propaleaetherium appear less alert than usual.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.980
Throughout the afternoon, they have continued to eat the food.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.580
fermenting grapes off the ground.

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:04.740
They contain only the smallest amount of alcohol,

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:08.610
but it is enough to dull their usually sharp senses.

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:21.420
This is a bad time to get careless.

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:13.400
the leptictidium scurry to safety just.

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:42.140
This is a world where birds eat horses.

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:54.140
Dusk, the Gastornis returns to her nest.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:57.870
Only now does she discover the fate of her offspring.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:06.660
The biggest predator here, humbled by the smallest.

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:17.440
Her size forces her to nest on the ground, and now she has paid the price.

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:20.550
Months of dedication wasted.

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:38.180
Despite everything, this evening the Leptictitium have fed well and all the young have survived their first outing.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:43.500
The night is usually a safe time for them. Tonight will be different.

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:13.180
After a day of slumber, this nocturnal god Inosha is stirring.

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:17.210
This is a type of primate, a new group of mammals

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:19.250
that specialize in living in trees.

00:23:20.290 --> 00:23:24.010
Primates possess keen vision and amazingly dexterous hands,

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:27.000
which not only help them to move among the branches,

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:29.920
but also to wheedle out their favorite

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:37.000
insects even when they're beneath the bark. They are inventive and resourceful

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:41.910
animals and although these godinotia are solitary, primates have a sociable

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:46.850
future. Among their descendants in millions of years time will be a hairless

00:23:46.850 --> 00:23:57.740
type of ape called man. The lakeside the Ambulocetus is still maintaining his

00:23:57.740 --> 00:23:59.970
patient vigil, waiting for prey.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:13.000
At night, a whole different cast of animals comes down to the water's edge, and eventually,

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:16.900
a small predator chooses the wrong bit of lake to drink from.

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:46.260
The ambulocetus' killing technique is simple.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:51.840
His vice-like jaws hold the struggling prey until it drowns.

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:25.940
The dark hours go by and some of the Godinotia are becoming less solitary.

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:29.980
Copulation among these primates is all...

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:56.050
part of the normal rhythm of the night but this is not a normal night tremor

00:25:56.050 --> 00:25:59.830
these are common here but this one is

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:08.250
The gas at the bottom of the lake is suddenly shaken free,

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:11.510
releasing a deadly cloud of carbon dioxide.

00:26:39.950 --> 00:26:46.290
Driven by the wind, the gas sweeps through the forest undergrowth, suffocating every animal in its path.

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:09.630
The day has come full circle.

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:14.400
The poisonous cloud has cleared, leaving a trail of corpses.

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:26.720
The Leptictidium, however, was lucky.

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:29.330
She and her litter escaped death.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:35.810
because their nest lay away from the path of the deadly gas, but her struggle to survive

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:47.430
never stops. It's time to hunt again. Once more, the Ambulocetus is lying in her path,

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:55.090
but she instinctively knows he is dead. Today, the Leptictidium is the survivor, but ironically,

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:59.930
the future holds a different outcome for these mammals. The Leptictidium and her kind are

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.820
destined to become extinct when this hot, wet world gets cooler and drier and the world's

00:28:05.820 --> 00:28:12.910
forests start to disappear. Ambulocetus is the one with the big future. He is the ancestor

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:18.830
of the whales, the most magnificent dynasty of mammals, and mammals are about to take

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:28.340
over the world. Next time, we move forward 15 million years to when the descendants of

00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:29.980
the Ambulocetus become the...

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:39.400
true masters of the oceans. On land too, mammals shrug off their small beginnings to evolve

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:43.250
into the largest and fiercest animals on the planet.

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:55.690
When the dinosaurs disappeared, so too did the gigantic marine reptiles that once terrorized the oceans.

00:29:56.940 --> 00:29:59.980
For almost 25 million years, there was...

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:09.120
nothing around to eat the sharks. But there are now awesome new monsters of

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:27.530
the deep. Giant whales have arrived. Forget the gentle filter feeders of the 21st

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:29.250
century. These days

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:56.640
every whale is a killer it is the late eocene and the world is still a hot one however it

00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:59.980
is drier than before and so where once the land was completed

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:05.820
covered in lush rainforest there are now more open spaces freed from the

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:10.130
constraints of living in dense forest some mammals have started to get larger

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:22.450
here on the scrub plains big is beautiful these changes have not suited

00:31:22.450 --> 00:31:27.330
everyone the large killer birds are no longer much in evidence replaced on

00:31:27.330 --> 00:31:29.690
most continents by fearsome new mammal

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:41.010
predators. Like dinosaurs before them, mammals dominate the planet. But they are about to

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:47.580
undergo their severest challenge. The climate change the world has seen so far is mild

00:31:47.830 --> 00:31:57.400
compared to what is coming. This programme is about the beginning of climate chaos.

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:06.110
The problem starts not on land, but in the ocean, and affects even the mightiest of mammals.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:14.850
This is the legendary Tethys, a tropical sea that stretches almost halfway around the world

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:19.370
and has connected Asia with the Atlantic since the time of the dinosaurs.

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:24.300
It is now home to one of the mammals' biggest success stories.

00:32:35.690 --> 00:32:44.530
Bacillosaurus, 18 meters of predatory whale, four times the length of a great white shark.

00:32:45.250 --> 00:32:47.290
This female weighs 60 tons.

00:32:51.980 --> 00:32:59.800
Incredible to think then, ancestors were tiny, furry, shrew-like animals.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:01.320
that lived in trees.

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:20.110
At this time of year, off the coast of the northern Tethys,

00:33:21.190 --> 00:33:22.550
Bacillosaurus gather to mate.

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:38.280
A female is being eagerly pursued by several males.

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:42.460
But it is the eldest and biggest male that she chooses to mate with.

00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:50.220
Mating is not an easy task for two such huge, free-floating animals.

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:52.330
They need a little extra help.

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:55.520
As the successful male maneuvers into position,

00:33:55.760 --> 00:33:59.880
he calls upon one small legacy of their distant land ancestors.

00:34:07.570 --> 00:34:10.350
Basilosaurus have retained two tiny back legs.

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:13.000
These are useless for walking or even swimming,

00:34:13.260 --> 00:34:17.560
but they are used to help lock their long narrow bodies together during mating.

00:34:18.620 --> 00:34:21.610
The whales of the future will lose these legs altogether.

00:34:42.730 --> 00:34:51.390
Courtship is over, but as it will turn out, the natural disaster that is looming is about to make things very tough indeed for marine life.

00:34:55.680 --> 00:34:59.760
This female's fate is being influenced by events far, far away.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:06.000
in the Antarctic. For the first time in hundreds of millions of years, the sea is freezing at the

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:14.430
poles, throwing ocean currents into turmoil. For a whale that needs on average 80 kilograms of food

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:21.120
a day, the slightest change in fish stocks is bad news. She is at the top of a food chain that is

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:24.270
about to collapse, and she has just become pregnant.

00:35:36.200 --> 00:35:39.440
The ocean currents are also starting to disrupt the climate

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:43.230
and have already affected weather patterns along the Tethys coastline.

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:48.730
Many areas used to high rainfall have suffered prolonged drought this year.

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:57.680
Despite these problems, life ploughs on as best it can.

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:05.150
But the drought can have some pretty nasty side effects.

00:36:06.050 --> 00:36:11.420
This is Androsarchus, a huge carnivore as tall as a horse and weighing close to a ton.

00:36:12.310 --> 00:36:17.800
Normally he wanders inland in search of food, but the drought has driven him onto the beach.

00:36:19.440 --> 00:36:24.200
Bad news for the exhausted turtles struggling back to the water after a night laying eggs.

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:29.940
First, the Androsarchus seems a little unsure of what...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:39.140
do with these curious shelled creatures. He is more used to picking over the carcasses

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:48.330
of giant herbivores. Despite appearances, Androsarchus is not related to modern scavengers like dogs

00:36:48.330 --> 00:36:56.700
or hyenas. Bizarrely, he has hooves on his feet instead of claws. In fact, his nearest

00:36:56.700 --> 00:36:59.840
modern relatives are hoofed animals like sheep and goats.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:08.500
He is, in a sense, a sheep in wolf's clothing.

00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:13.440
He is also the largest mammal carnivore ever to walk the earth.

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:17.460
His huge one-meter-long jaws are designed to crush anything,

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:21.210
so the turtle's main defense is of little use.

00:37:31.170 --> 00:37:39.630
During droughts scavengers often do well but in the long run the hoofed

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:44.210
predators will not adapt quickly enough to changes in the climate. He is the last

00:37:44.210 --> 00:37:55.150
of a dying breed. Months have passed and the erratic ocean currents have disturbed

00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:59.530
the fish stock so much that the female Bacillosaurus is searching for food

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:05.880
of miles from her usual hunting grounds four months pregnant her situation has

00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:12.220
become critical she is now forced to hunt in the most unlikely places lining

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:19.680
the southern edges of the tethys are endless expanses of mangrove swamp in

00:38:28.720 --> 00:38:29.440
the ear scene

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.750
It's a vast, thriving network of waterways, but believe it or not, you are looking at

00:38:34.750 --> 00:38:39.700
what will become one of the driest areas on the planet, the Sahara Desert.

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:53.580
It's hardly a classic hunting ground for an ocean-going whale, but she is desperate,

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.670
and there is prey here of a sort.

00:39:00.540 --> 00:39:07.610
In the labyrinth of tidal channels, her size is a real handicap, but hunger draws her in.

00:39:10.730 --> 00:39:13.030
Watching from the branches above are primates.

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:19.920
These are apidium that live in highly social groups, and word quickly gets around when a threat is spotted.

00:39:28.860 --> 00:39:29.880
Other creatures here...

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:31.660
are oblivious to the new danger.

00:39:34.620 --> 00:39:36.640
The amphibious mammal, Myrotherium,

00:39:36.880 --> 00:39:39.380
is too large to be bothered by the usual predators,

00:39:39.770 --> 00:39:43.740
such as crocodiles, so they ignore the chattering primates

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:46.200
and return to the business of eating.

00:39:48.140 --> 00:39:50.140
The Apidium move away from the channel

00:39:50.140 --> 00:39:52.200
and continue through the mangroves

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:53.700
looking for fruiting trees.

00:39:54.540 --> 00:39:56.800
Because different trees fruit at different times,

00:39:57.060 --> 00:39:59.200
they often have to cross the waterways.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:06.410
a very risky activity. There are sharks and crocodiles to worry about, and now there is

00:40:06.410 --> 00:40:25.620
a whale as well. It's a leap of faith, but leaping is one thing Epidium are very good at.

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:33.560
The whale is frustrated for the moment.

00:40:42.310 --> 00:40:43.870
With the rising tide, however,

00:40:44.150 --> 00:40:46.530
some waterways become too wide to jump,

00:40:46.630 --> 00:40:49.550
and the epidium have to find more hazardous ways across,

00:40:49.750 --> 00:40:50.850
closer to the water.

00:41:08.090 --> 00:41:09.890
Today, their nightmare came true.

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:12.270
It was a shark.

00:41:16.410 --> 00:41:17.810
They certainly won't cross now.

00:41:18.440 --> 00:41:20.220
They'll have to wait for the tide to go down.

00:41:36.810 --> 00:41:40.130
By contrast, the water isn't usually dangerous for the Myrotherium.

00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:42.220
They spend most of their day here.

00:41:45.190 --> 00:41:48.110
Although they are shaped like hippos and look a bit like pigs,

00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:50.470
Myrotherium are related to neither.

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:57.090
Look closely, though, and the Myrotherium's nose betrays its true family connection.

00:41:57.760 --> 00:41:59.980
The nostrils and lip have joined together to become...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:03.780
one dextrous muscular unit which helps them forage for food.

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:06.480
This is in fact a type of trunk.

00:42:07.690 --> 00:42:10.750
These benign herbivores are early relatives of the elephant.

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:16.510
At around 200 kilograms, they are too big for the sharks.

00:42:20.150 --> 00:42:22.550
One merotherium heads off for pastures new,

00:42:25.700 --> 00:42:27.400
but he is heading straight for the jaws

00:42:27.400 --> 00:42:28.980
of the female bacillosaurus.

00:42:43.370 --> 00:42:46.230
Myrotherium scrabbles onto dry land just in time.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:03.000
But the hungry Bacillosaurus isn't about to give up yet.

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:29.980
Dry land and safety are only temporary things in the map.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:43.620
mangroves. The water still has some way to rise and most solid ground will become seabed in just

00:43:43.620 --> 00:43:59.860
a few hours time as the high tide sweeps in. Bacillosaurus will soon be able to reach the

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:01.140
miratherium.

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:37.510
In her desperation, she has attacked too early and run aground.

00:45:00.090 --> 00:45:04.870
By the time the whale has worked herself free, the Myrotherium has escaped to the shallower channels,

00:45:05.270 --> 00:45:07.790
where even the starving Bacillosaurus won't follow.

00:45:08.410 --> 00:45:12.460
For the whale, the mangroves are turning out to be no better than the open sea.

00:45:19.240 --> 00:45:22.160
The Eocene El Nino continues to wreak havoc.

00:45:23.120 --> 00:45:25.740
The weather patterns that animals rely on are confused.

00:45:26.710 --> 00:45:29.980
In the northern Tethys, the rains have come, but...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.600
six weeks later than usual.

00:45:31.860 --> 00:45:43.730
And then instead of lasting months,

00:45:44.330 --> 00:45:45.870
they are over in a few weeks.

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:55.850
In the scrubland, the rains have finally provided

00:45:55.850 --> 00:45:59.400
new growth, but the damage caused by the prolonged drought

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:01.070
has already been done.

00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:06.800
These Brontothea herds in particular have been hit hard.

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:09.780
Though distantly related to horses and rhinos,

00:46:10.110 --> 00:46:11.230
they're not much like either.

00:46:11.980 --> 00:46:13.960
They are twice as big as modern rhinos,

00:46:14.060 --> 00:46:16.400
with brains just one third of the size.

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:18.780
They are not the brightest of beasts.

00:46:22.460 --> 00:46:24.360
Still, they are one of the most successful groups

00:46:24.360 --> 00:46:27.530
of mammals around, found across the northern hemisphere

00:46:27.530 --> 00:46:28.710
in herds of hundreds.

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:33.070
This year, though, some herds are in a sorry state.

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:43.660
This should be a time for calving, but a high proportion are being stillborn.

00:46:47.880 --> 00:46:52.540
The few youngsters that have survived the drought continue to behave as youngsters always do.

00:46:55.250 --> 00:46:59.980
These two adolescent males practice for adulthood, challenging one another over...

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:01.620
as yet non-existent females.

00:47:04.780 --> 00:47:06.580
In another difference to rhinos,

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:09.660
the outgrowths on their noses are not horn or hair,

00:47:09.960 --> 00:47:10.580
but bone.

00:47:11.220 --> 00:47:13.040
These are more for show than for head-butting.

00:47:13.410 --> 00:47:15.830
The bone crests are far too brittle for that.

00:47:16.340 --> 00:47:22.490
The scavengers are having a better time of it,

00:47:28.200 --> 00:47:29.960
and Androsarchus is trying to harrow

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:47.430
a mother Brontothea and get at her dead calf the female though is being fiercely protective it is

00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:52.280
impossible to say whether this mother even understands that her calf is dead like most

00:47:52.280 --> 00:47:57.680
mammals she has a very strong bond with her offspring and will defend it for as long as she can

00:48:04.480 --> 00:48:06.580
Now a second Androsarchus has arrived.

00:48:07.260 --> 00:48:09.940
It is going to be difficult for the mother to keep them both away.

00:48:25.430 --> 00:48:27.910
First Androsarchus seizes its chance.

00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:43.960
After all day defending a lost cause,

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:46.020
the mother appears to be giving up.

00:48:56.440 --> 00:48:59.180
The scavengers aren't used to having to share the spoils.

00:49:01.020 --> 00:49:18.480
As they wrestle for the calf, the mother mistakes its movement for signs of life.

00:49:24.140 --> 00:49:25.780
She is spurred into action again.

00:49:48.300 --> 00:49:50.800
She returns to her lonely and fruitless vigil.

00:49:54.320 --> 00:49:55.840
The Androsarchus will be back.

00:50:10.300 --> 00:50:14.880
As the weeks pass, the environmental crisis at sea shows no sign of easing.

00:50:15.680 --> 00:50:18.630
It can only be described as an ocean famine.

00:50:24.450 --> 00:50:28.880
For the female bacillosaurus who is now heavily pregnant, things are desperate.

00:50:30.890 --> 00:50:34.950
She is getting barely enough for herself to eat, never mind her unborn calf.

00:50:35.410 --> 00:50:41.970
With her limited fat reserves all but gone, her body will soon abort her offspring in order to preserve her own life.

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:57.580
Suddenly the sea around her is filled with the bustling activity of a group of smaller whales called Doradon.

00:51:04.190 --> 00:51:08.490
When Doradon gather in numbers, it means that the females are about to give birth.

00:51:11.670 --> 00:51:15.230
This might look like a friendly greeting. It is anything but.

00:51:16.220 --> 00:51:20.870
The smaller whales are mobbing the giant, using their numbers to try and force her away.

00:51:21.940 --> 00:51:27.180
Basilosaurus preys on their young, and somewhere nearby must be the Doradon's carving ground.

00:51:33.100 --> 00:51:38.100
It appears as though she has been driven to the seabed, but there is method in her madness.

00:51:43.410 --> 00:51:48.350
She has to do something that is more important than ever if her unborn calf is to survive.

00:51:49.460 --> 00:51:50.440
She must scratch.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:05.040
By dragging herself over a convenient sandbar, she can slough off the outer layer of her skin

00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:09.140
and with it, any parasites or barnacles that may have got a foothold.

00:52:16.280 --> 00:52:21.700
For an animal that relies on speed to hunt, keeping her body sleek and streamlined is

00:52:21.820 --> 00:52:24.410
the difference between success and hunger.

00:52:25.580 --> 00:52:27.640
She is ready to go hunting whales.

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:46.400
Every year, Dorodon gather in this calm, protected lagoon to give birth.

00:52:49.380 --> 00:52:56.820
The first calves are appearing just a few days old and already in mortal danger.

00:52:57.620 --> 00:52:59.960
The Bacillosaurus has found the lagoon.

00:53:06.010 --> 00:53:10.160
She has been spotted, and the calves are quickly shepherded away from the threat.

00:53:24.810 --> 00:53:29.570
Defending together, the adult Dorodon launch aggressive attacks against the Bacillosaurus.

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:38.980
attempt to drive her off. Their efforts appear surprisingly effective. The

00:53:38.980 --> 00:53:56.520
Bacillosaurus moves away. Calm is restored but it won't last for long. This

00:53:56.520 --> 00:53:57.480
is a hungry mother.

00:54:28.060 --> 00:54:29.980
Of the next few days, the bacillus saw.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:31.960
returns time after time.

00:54:38.840 --> 00:54:40.860
Goon is transformed from a sanctuary

00:54:40.860 --> 00:54:42.540
into a bloodbath.

00:54:47.830 --> 00:54:50.310
This is what it means to be top of the food chain.

00:54:51.100 --> 00:54:52.480
It has taken the death of several

00:54:52.480 --> 00:54:54.990
Dorodon calves to further the survival

00:54:54.990 --> 00:54:57.120
of the unborn Basilosaurus.

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:35.470
It has been a year since the female Basilosaurus mated.

00:55:36.130 --> 00:55:38.150
She is once again out in the open seas.

00:55:42.300 --> 00:55:44.770
But this time, she is not alone.

00:55:52.480 --> 00:55:54.940
Against all the odds, she has finally given birth.

00:56:04.440 --> 00:56:08.560
Against the odds, she and her calf have survived.

00:56:17.060 --> 00:56:21.500
Sadly, what they have been through is merely the start of the climate chaos to follow,

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:27.970
which will end the Eocene period and cause the largest extinction since the death of the dinosaurs.

00:56:30.730 --> 00:56:38.240
As devastating as any meteor will be the catastrophic disruption of the ocean's currents caused by the gradual freezing of Antarctica.

00:56:39.150 --> 00:56:46.820
Like a gigantic El Nino, only much, much worse, 20% of living things on Earth will die out.

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:55.900
Whales as a group will survive, but Bacillosaurus and her kind will not be among them.

00:57:02.040 --> 00:57:05.550
Next time, mammals on land go from big to bigger.

00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:09.720
We will walk with the Indrocathias,

00:57:10.120 --> 00:57:13.400
mammals that rival in size the dinosaurs of old.

00:57:13.930 --> 00:57:19.080
It is a world of the big, the bad, and the ugly.

00:58:31.500 --> 00:58:37.120
Out of the darkness emerges a 12-ton giant.

00:58:38.020 --> 00:58:41.370
She is an Indra-Kathir and she is about to give birth.

00:58:43.720 --> 00:58:48.990
She seeks out a secluded spot in a canyon where she hopes the newborn will be safe from predators.

00:58:52.040 --> 00:58:58.230
It is now just before dawn and at last her two-year pregnancy is coming to an end.

00:59:08.120 --> 00:59:11.130
Garf is a male, and he is already in trouble.

00:59:17.210 --> 00:59:19.250
The noise and smell of the mother's labor

00:59:19.250 --> 00:59:22.080
has attracted the predators, Hyenadon.

00:59:23.390 --> 00:59:26.260
Despite the name, these are not relatives of hyenas.

00:59:26.740 --> 00:59:28.800
These are more dangerous by far.

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:35.220
to the Indrocathia they may look small but these carnivores are as big as rhinos and they are

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:43.590
easily capable of killing the calf if they can get past his mother. As the predators circle,

00:59:44.360 --> 00:59:48.770
she desperately tries to keep the baby between her legs where she can defend him with powerful

00:59:48.770 --> 00:59:59.970
kicks. The hyenodon keep coming and...

01:00:01.860 --> 01:00:07.010
This calf will be lucky to see his first sunrise.

01:00:24.240 --> 01:00:26.260
It is a time called the Oligocene.

01:00:26.940 --> 01:00:29.980
The world has just been through years of catastrophic clim-

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:35.880
change that led to the extinction of one species in five. Earth has since bounced

01:00:35.880 --> 01:00:40.230
back but in the process it's become a changed world, different weather,

01:00:40.780 --> 01:00:49.500
different plants and different animals. Here in Mongolia some animals have grown

01:00:49.500 --> 01:00:56.130
enormous. This program will follow the fate of just one of these giants, a baby

01:00:56.130 --> 01:00:59.960
Indrekathir. During the night this canyon was the scene of

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:08.190
dramatic birth but it seems his mother saw off the hyenodon he is a very lucky calf as a newborn

01:01:08.190 --> 01:01:14.850
he already weighs a quarter of a ton and his legs aren't yet used to bearing any weight at all so

01:01:15.130 --> 01:01:24.700
he'll spend the rest of the morning learning how to walk around him the other residents

01:01:24.700 --> 01:01:29.340
of the canyon are starting to stir strange beasts like the caliceth is

01:01:30.250 --> 01:01:34.430
Almost three meters tall, they walk on their knuckles to protect their long claws.

01:01:34.790 --> 01:01:38.410
These powerful limbs are very effective defensive weapons,

01:01:38.590 --> 01:01:44.560
but more often these herbivores use them to hook down branches to get at the softest leaves.

01:01:49.580 --> 01:01:55.990
They walk like gorillas and eat like pandas, but their closest modern relatives are actually horses.

01:02:02.620 --> 01:02:05.320
The newborn calf also has food on his mind.

01:02:06.310 --> 01:02:09.030
This is the beginning of the most vulnerable period of his life.

01:02:09.800 --> 01:02:12.680
He'll be totally dependent on his mother for at least three years.

01:02:13.380 --> 01:02:17.410
He needs her protection, and for the first year he also relies on her milk.

01:02:18.060 --> 01:02:21.040
This is an astonishingly long commitment for any mother,

01:02:21.680 --> 01:02:23.620
but if he can just survive that long,

01:02:24.050 --> 01:02:28.350
his size will mean there will not be a predator on earth that can touch him.

01:02:33.130 --> 01:02:36.670
In the Oligocene, large parts of the world have become highly seasonal.

01:02:37.480 --> 01:02:42.240
Here in Mongolia, long dry seasons are broken by short, violent wet seasons.

01:02:43.010 --> 01:02:46.750
The climate has shaped not only the environment, but also the animals.

01:02:49.950 --> 01:02:55.140
It is survival of the meanest, and they don't come much meaner than this.

01:02:55.760 --> 01:02:58.830
A giant entelodont, the bully of the plains.

01:03:00.190 --> 01:03:02.050
These are distant relatives of the pig.

01:03:02.730 --> 01:03:06.140
Two metres tall, aggressive and built like tanks,

01:03:07.080 --> 01:03:09.620
but with a brain no bigger than an orange.

01:03:12.110 --> 01:03:15.230
The bear dog mother and cubs retreat to a safer distance,

01:03:15.700 --> 01:03:17.100
but it isn't going to be their day.

01:03:17.700 --> 01:03:21.380
Although these relatives of both bears and dogs are vicious little predators,

01:03:21.880 --> 01:03:24.350
at the waterhole, it's size that matters.

01:03:28.120 --> 01:03:29.980
The mother Indra-Kathir has also come...

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:39.970
here to drink, but her calf is nowhere to be seen. A second male entelodont

01:03:39.970 --> 01:03:44.070
arrives at the waterhole. During the mating season, these huge scavengers get

01:03:44.070 --> 01:03:48.250
very aggressive towards each other as they try to establish who is top hog.

01:03:49.870 --> 01:03:59.960
Entelodonts are their own worst enemy. It is enough to put off even an Indricothere.

01:04:00.410 --> 01:04:00.610
Thank you.

01:04:32.360 --> 01:04:34.420
The Intellidont's fearsome set of teeth

01:04:34.420 --> 01:04:37.600
have inflicted deep wounds on the loser.

01:04:44.670 --> 01:04:47.410
Back in the canyon, the calf lies motionless.

01:04:48.410 --> 01:04:52.040
He is playing dead, until disturbed, that is.

01:05:04.160 --> 01:05:29.960
This is the young calf's survival strategy. In his mother's absence, he instinctively lies still to avoid the attentions of predators. Only when she eventually returns does he come out of hiding. He remains...

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:36.170
within the comparative safety of the canyon walls for another week but then his mother decides it is

01:05:36.170 --> 01:05:49.720
time to leave. Kaff is now strong and sure-footed enough to venture out onto the open plains where

01:05:50.070 --> 01:05:58.590
he will spend the rest of his life. This is where the true giants roam.

01:06:02.200 --> 01:06:05.520
Not since the dinosaurs has nature got this big.

01:06:08.920 --> 01:06:10.980
Indrocathias are related to rhinos,

01:06:11.320 --> 01:06:13.220
but their size is in a different league.

01:06:14.220 --> 01:06:16.860
Fully grown male stands over seven meters tall

01:06:16.860 --> 01:06:19.040
and weighs in at 15 tons.

01:06:19.700 --> 01:06:22.300
That's equivalent to eight modern rhinos.

01:06:23.220 --> 01:06:25.760
No other land animal even comes close.

01:06:31.440 --> 01:06:38.620
Being big not only protects them from predators, but in this dry land allows them to go without food or water for days.

01:06:44.100 --> 01:06:50.610
The calf is eager to explore this big new world, but his first encounter is a shock for him.

01:06:52.380 --> 01:06:55.340
An aggressive bear dog has no time for his curiosity.

01:06:56.050 --> 01:06:59.800
Her cubs are nearby, and to her...

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:01.620
The gawky calf is a threat.

01:07:06.140 --> 01:07:08.720
Doesn't take much to send him running back to his mother.

01:07:19.360 --> 01:07:22.400
Right from the beginning, the calf starts copying his mother

01:07:22.400 --> 01:07:24.320
to learn how to deal with the world.

01:07:25.040 --> 01:07:27.640
So when she arrives in her usual feeding area

01:07:27.640 --> 01:07:29.360
and sniffs a pile of dung,

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:30.880
He does the same.

01:07:32.040 --> 01:07:36.160
One day he will understand that since every Indrocathia has a unique odour,

01:07:36.580 --> 01:07:39.720
this is how his mother tells which other animals are in the area.

01:07:40.670 --> 01:07:42.860
His lessons in survival have begun.

01:07:48.360 --> 01:07:52.300
Nearby is a juvenile Indrocathia, just over three years old.

01:07:55.280 --> 01:07:58.200
This is no stranger but his mother's previous calf.

01:07:59.050 --> 01:07:59.970
However, she has no...

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:06.240
time for him now and violently repels him he doesn't know it but our calf is

01:08:06.240 --> 01:08:23.040
looking at his own future the long dry season is just starting but there is

01:08:23.040 --> 01:08:27.130
still plenty of food around in another month or so the situation will be very

01:08:27.130 --> 01:08:29.740
different and so animals are gorging themselves

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:37.840
while the going is good. As the mother browses she has the tops of the trees to

01:08:37.840 --> 01:08:48.460
herself. It's another advantage of being big. Only two months old the calf doesn't

01:08:48.460 --> 01:08:53.070
need to eat. He is still living off his mother's milk but it doesn't stop him

01:08:53.070 --> 01:08:57.760
trying to imitate her. This is how calves learn which plants are safe to eat.

01:09:02.650 --> 01:09:05.690
However, this is a bad time for him to wander off on his own.

01:09:06.440 --> 01:09:09.140
Just 20 meters away, a hyenodon is stalking.

01:09:37.420 --> 01:09:42.380
Hyenodon's jaws have a bone-shattering force of over 1,000 pounds per square inch.

01:09:43.190 --> 01:09:45.410
One bite breaks the calicothea's neck.

01:09:51.800 --> 01:09:55.850
They are capable of eating everything on a carcass, even the teeth.

01:10:03.450 --> 01:10:07.950
today he isn't going to get the chance the local bullies have arrived and he

01:10:07.950 --> 01:10:25.660
is outnumbered in a futile attempt to hide the smell of the carcass the hyena

01:10:25.660 --> 01:10:29.820
Don defecates on it but it will take more than that to put off the intelligence

01:10:57.410 --> 01:10:59.620
hogs from hell have won the prize.

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:14.620
The dry season has transformed the landscape.

01:11:15.390 --> 01:11:18.210
It's as if the whole lifeblood has drained out of the plains.

01:11:19.150 --> 01:11:21.350
This is what it means to be a seasonal world.

01:11:22.100 --> 01:11:25.340
It hasn't rained for months, there is no greenery left to eat,

01:11:25.760 --> 01:11:27.260
and there is precious little to drink.

01:11:28.240 --> 01:11:29.980
Here in Mongolia, mammals have evolved...

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:34.820
to cope with even these kinds of conditions but at this time of year they

01:11:35.160 --> 01:11:49.340
are put under their greatest stress. The mother Indrakathia has not found water

01:11:49.340 --> 01:11:54.730
for several days and in her dehydrated state she is not producing milk. The

01:11:54.730 --> 01:11:59.110
calf is now nine months old and it is his first taste of true hardship.

01:12:03.670 --> 01:12:15.100
Despite his repeated attempts to suckle, the mother rejects him.

01:12:22.440 --> 01:12:26.080
Because of their size, adults can survive without water for days.

01:12:26.630 --> 01:12:29.970
But the calf, being smaller, has fewer reserves to draw on.

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:51.940
and is getting dangerously weak the nights are cooler and a far better time

01:12:51.940 --> 01:12:58.590
for them to move around so every evening they set off in search of water other

01:12:58.590 --> 01:12:59.910
indra kathias are on the same

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:06.630
journey and tonight the pair is joined by an older female. Indra-Kathias can

01:13:06.630 --> 01:13:10.810
easily live into their 80s and this longevity gives them a unique knowledge

01:13:10.810 --> 01:13:20.260
of their environment. If there is any water nearby it's a good bet the old

01:13:20.260 --> 01:13:21.780
female knows where it is.

01:13:30.540 --> 01:13:40.310
At last they find the muddy remnant of a lake and just in time because the calf is close to collapse

01:13:40.310 --> 01:13:43.020
He will have to wait a while for his milk

01:13:43.020 --> 01:13:59.980
But at least he can now quench his thirst it is the first night for some time that these giants can lie down

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:18.800
and get a proper sleep the next morning the mother receives an early call the

01:14:19.370 --> 01:14:26.510
calf is still starving but this time she responds and he is rewarded with milk

01:14:41.230 --> 01:14:44.720
When the rains eventually come, they come down hard.

01:14:49.630 --> 01:14:53.660
From parched plains to a slippery quagmire,

01:14:54.060 --> 01:14:56.990
everything changes in an instant.

01:15:15.900 --> 01:15:18.540
but the sudden downpours bring disaster for some.

01:15:19.320 --> 01:15:21.950
This bear dog's den has been undermined

01:15:21.950 --> 01:15:23.750
by the flood waters and collapsed.

01:15:24.850 --> 01:15:27.710
An anxious mother is forced to dig to find her cubs.

01:15:34.140 --> 01:15:38.940
She finds only one, and it is already dead.

01:15:43.770 --> 01:15:46.530
There is a river now, where none existed before.

01:15:48.280 --> 01:15:51.310
In their search for food, the pair must cross it.

01:15:55.530 --> 01:15:59.960
This is a whole new ordeal for the calf, and at first, he is very relaxed.

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:14.810
to follow his mom into the water in the end though it is obvious that he is more

01:16:14.810 --> 01:16:24.390
frightened of being left behind than he is of crossing the river this is the

01:16:24.390 --> 01:16:26.510
first swim of his life unfortunately

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:54.060
It turns out to be the easy bit when the calf gets to the other side

01:16:54.060 --> 01:16:59.980
He still has to get to the top of the opposite bank. It is steep and slippery and not

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:02.720
Unlike his mum, he isn't going to make it in one small step,

01:17:02.800 --> 01:17:06.310
but there is nothing his mother can do to help him.

01:17:10.680 --> 01:17:14.220
He is on his own, and he doesn't like it.

01:17:30.860 --> 01:17:50.830
It's not an easy task for such a heavy baby.

01:18:00.480 --> 01:18:07.120
The drought might have been a tough time, but in many ways the coming of the rains marks the end of the good life for the calf.

01:18:08.140 --> 01:18:10.180
It is time for him to feed himself.

01:18:11.560 --> 01:18:20.740
Indricothere calves are weaned off milk at about a year old, but having known little else but milk for his whole life, he needs quite a lot of persuading to give it up.

01:18:24.800 --> 01:18:28.840
Try as he might to get some of his favourite tipple, mum is having none of it.

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:45.280
have to eat his greens or go without. After the long dry season color slowly

01:18:45.280 --> 01:18:51.500
returns to the plains as the cycle of life starts over again and for the

01:18:51.500 --> 01:18:59.920
Indrocathias it is time for mating. A young male has caught the scent

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:07.810
of a receptive female. It is the calf's mother who is fertile again. Now she has stopped suckling

01:19:07.810 --> 01:19:16.860
her calf. The calf plainly has no idea what to make of this approaching colossus and at this

01:19:16.860 --> 01:19:27.500
point his mother shows no interest either. Then a second older male appears on the scene. He

01:19:27.500 --> 01:19:29.980
emphasizes his status by spraying the ground.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:41.610
liberally with urine. The younger male isn't so easily intimidated and the two

01:19:41.610 --> 01:19:57.010
rivals size each other up before starting a brutal contest. 30 tons worth

01:19:57.010 --> 01:19:59.970
of Indra-Kathir fight, the forces involved are...

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:04.580
bone-shattering the skulls of males are specially built to withstand these

01:20:04.580 --> 01:20:18.210
contests it takes half an hour of abuse before the older male backs down but

01:20:25.340 --> 01:20:29.970
with the fight over the calf is now at serious risk the biggest threat

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:47.870
to a young Indra Kathir is an adult male during mating calves can get trampled to death it is the

01:20:47.870 --> 01:20:53.410
first time that another individual has come between him and his mother in his distress he

01:20:53.470 --> 01:20:56.560
tries desperately to get in the way to stop the mating

01:21:10.560 --> 01:21:14.980
Over the next 36 hours, the male will mate with the calf's mother several more times,

01:21:15.060 --> 01:21:17.140
preventing any other males from doing so.

01:21:18.020 --> 01:21:19.940
Our calf is in for a tough time.

01:21:41.520 --> 01:21:46.650
It's the very beginning of the wet season, and the Kala Kathias have started to search out new growth.

01:21:53.250 --> 01:21:58.210
The mother Indra Kathia is expecting. She is, in fact, only days from giving birth.

01:22:01.240 --> 01:22:05.930
Standing by her is the calf, now three years old and weighing over a ton.

01:22:06.840 --> 01:22:10.340
He has obviously learnt many of the tricks of Indrakathir life from his mother,

01:22:11.040 --> 01:22:14.000
like using trees to scratch the parasites off his back.

01:22:17.380 --> 01:22:22.910
However, nothing he has learnt so far will prepare him for what is about to happen.

01:22:23.850 --> 01:22:27.990
In the morning, the calf will suffer the biggest upset of his life.

01:22:30.670 --> 01:22:50.980
The mother is behaving very oddly indeed towards her calf but she is only doing what she has to do.

01:22:53.250 --> 01:22:59.900
She is chasing him away to give her unborn calf.

01:23:00.030 --> 01:23:05.300
the best chance in life, she must sever the three-year-old bond she has had with the male

01:23:05.300 --> 01:23:16.200
calf. It is time for him to make his own way in the world. This is the hardest lesson of all.

01:23:39.340 --> 01:23:47.650
For some time, the calf disappears, only to turn up a couple of weeks later, alone, on a riverbank.

01:23:50.750 --> 01:23:57.670
The cause of his limp is unclear, but the most likely explanation is that he has had a run-in with a young adult, Indrokathia,

01:23:58.170 --> 01:23:59.970
one of the few creatures that can threaten him.

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:04.500
and now he is heading back to the only safety he knows.

01:24:04.700 --> 01:24:11.770
His mother has a new calf in tow

01:24:11.770 --> 01:24:15.140
and her maternal instincts have been transferred to the newborn.

01:24:21.160 --> 01:24:23.120
To her, he is now just a threat.

01:24:35.440 --> 01:24:37.920
He is never again going to have the protection of his mother.

01:24:38.590 --> 01:24:41.210
The rest of his life he will spend alone.

01:24:49.890 --> 01:24:56.000
One month, two months, three months go by and there is no sign of the calf.

01:24:57.420 --> 01:24:59.940
Then suddenly, one day he reappears.

01:25:00.410 --> 01:25:05.290
Back in his old territory, his injury has healed, and he seems in good health.

01:25:08.860 --> 01:25:11.260
But he is dangerously close to an entelodont.

01:25:25.390 --> 01:25:29.960
Any fears about his safety suddenly seem to be misplaced, because this injury...

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:33.400
is now big enough to look after himself.

01:25:53.120 --> 01:25:55.840
Next episode, prepare to come face to face

01:25:55.840 --> 01:25:58.960
with mankind's beginnings when we go back to Africa

01:25:58.960 --> 01:25:59.980
three million years ago.

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:03.400
ago to meet a species of ape that walked upright.

01:26:04.050 --> 01:26:06.810
Our ancestors lived in a dangerous world.

01:26:07.540 --> 01:26:10.970
Unlike us, they weren't predators, they were prey.

01:27:36.240 --> 01:27:38.850
Some animals can show emotion.

01:27:41.320 --> 01:27:44.900
These unique creatures are reacting to the death of one of their group

01:27:44.900 --> 01:27:47.560
with what can only be described as grief.

01:27:48.810 --> 01:27:53.030
It is the eldest female who has eventually lost her fight with malaria.

01:27:56.440 --> 01:27:59.980
Beside her body, her three-year-old orphan, his mother.

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:04.890
was the most important thing in his life and without her survival in a difficult

01:28:04.890 --> 01:28:24.040
world will become almost impossible. This is the northern end of the great

01:28:24.040 --> 01:28:29.970
African Rift Valley a staggering landscape created by fractures in the

01:28:30.060 --> 01:28:46.510
continent. This is the cradle of evolution for mankind. The world has been transformed in the

01:28:46.510 --> 01:28:52.380
last few million years by one small plant where once there was forest there are now vast tracts

01:28:52.380 --> 01:28:59.520
of grassland and with grass have come new species of grazing mammals.

01:29:05.560 --> 01:29:10.580
At first glance, you might think you were in the 21st century, but there are important differences.

01:29:11.460 --> 01:29:17.220
The big cats are actually of the saber-tooth variety, although these ones only have small sabers.

01:29:23.090 --> 01:29:29.880
And creatures like Dynotherium might look like elephants, but they are three times the size, and tusks that curve down.

01:29:30.160 --> 01:29:41.440
for stripping bark off trees but on the edge of the forest live the most

01:29:41.440 --> 01:29:50.100
remarkable animals of all type of ape has evolved that clearly shows the first

01:29:50.100 --> 01:29:56.660
signs of becoming more human what makes them closer to us than other apes is not

01:29:56.660 --> 01:29:59.630
their brains which are only one-third of the size

01:30:01.760 --> 01:30:04.140
It's not their skin, which is hairy.

01:30:04.220 --> 01:30:10.470
It's something they do that other apes just don't.

01:30:11.640 --> 01:30:13.830
Something that will one day lead them

01:30:13.830 --> 01:30:16.680
to be described as a missing link.

01:30:20.380 --> 01:30:22.740
These apes walk upright.

01:30:28.820 --> 01:30:29.980
These are Australopithecus.

01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:35.820
Go back 200,000 generations and your relatives would look something like this.

01:30:36.760 --> 01:30:42.420
They live in groups with complex social structures and are also very political animals.

01:30:47.580 --> 01:30:49.620
This is Grey, the eldest of the group.

01:30:50.340 --> 01:30:55.350
He is 30 years old and the top male, but only for as long as the females want him to be.

01:30:56.340 --> 01:30:59.980
Females are just two-thirds of the size of the males, but Grey needs to be...

01:31:00.000 --> 01:31:07.470
their support if he wants to stay in charge. Unfortunately, he's no longer

01:31:07.470 --> 01:31:23.080
getting it. With the death of the lead female, this noisy individual called

01:31:23.080 --> 01:31:26.920
Babel has now replaced her and she is no fan of Gray.

01:31:33.360 --> 01:31:40.760
bottom of the heap is the orphaned male called Blue. At three years old, his social skills

01:31:40.760 --> 01:31:49.740
are as yet undeveloped. With the death of his mother, he is becoming an outsider. But

01:31:49.740 --> 01:31:54.600
in truth, the whole group has problems. Last year, there were twelve of them. As a result

01:31:54.600 --> 01:31:59.970
of malaria, they now number just eight. This has led to...

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:20.710
within the hierarchy a younger larger male called Hercules is starting to

01:32:20.710 --> 01:32:21.510
challenge Gray

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:32.300
These fights are less violent than other primates.

01:32:32.740 --> 01:32:36.160
Baboons, for example, have large canines and bite each other.

01:32:37.180 --> 01:32:41.340
Australopithecus have small canines, and their fights are mostly shown.

01:32:53.440 --> 01:32:57.590
Gray has managed to put Hercules in his place, for now at least.

01:33:10.240 --> 01:33:16.110
One reason that our origins are here in Africa is that it's an ideal habitat for an upright ape,

01:33:16.760 --> 01:33:19.460
a patchwork of forest and savannah.

01:33:26.800 --> 01:33:29.960
Unlike many of the creatures here, the Australopithecus are...

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:32.880
comfortable in both the trees and the open spaces.

01:33:36.760 --> 01:33:39.200
They share the waterhole with Ancylotherium,

01:33:39.560 --> 01:33:42.220
an ancient type of animal that was once widespread,

01:33:42.800 --> 01:33:44.500
but is now only left in Africa.

01:33:49.480 --> 01:33:52.540
Fortunately, these herbivores are fairly harmless neighbors,

01:33:54.910 --> 01:33:57.210
which is more than you can say for Dynotherium.

01:33:57.840 --> 01:33:59.980
These are the biggest animals in the...

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:05.460
Australopithecus world. They are as tall as giraffes but weigh fourteen times as

01:34:05.460 --> 01:34:16.320
much. Even young dinotherium are worth avoiding.

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:33.160
In the weeks that follow, the males continue their squabbling.

01:34:41.550 --> 01:34:43.410
Blue, meanwhile, sits apart.

01:34:43.930 --> 01:34:46.050
He still has no idea how to integrate,

01:34:49.870 --> 01:34:52.290
but this is no time for the group to be divided.

01:35:00.800 --> 01:35:09.370
These males are from a larger, neighboring group.

01:35:09.830 --> 01:35:11.850
They are challenging Gray for this territory.

01:35:25.360 --> 01:35:29.960
Gray responds, but his group is now too few in number to fend off so many fools.

01:35:30.000 --> 01:35:30.720
grown males.

01:36:18.970 --> 01:36:25.870
group flees blue is in danger of getting left behind with no one to look after him

01:36:25.870 --> 01:36:29.970
he can only struggle to catch up without the group he won't so

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:59.590
Who has kept up but for his group things have gone from bad to worse they have been pushed out of a territory they have held

01:37:00.030 --> 01:37:08.300
For many seasons, establishing themselves in a new area won't be easy.

01:38:06.270 --> 01:38:08.770
Walking on two legs is an efficient way to travel

01:38:08.770 --> 01:38:11.880
and gives the Australopithecus a higher viewpoint

01:38:11.880 --> 01:38:12.860
than other apes.

01:38:13.660 --> 01:38:15.660
This means predators are easier to spot.

01:38:16.130 --> 01:38:19.410
Out here in the open, they must be especially vigilant.

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:31.980
The group also needs to stick together.

01:38:32.730 --> 01:38:35.030
Eight pairs of eyes are better than one.

01:38:35.250 --> 01:38:52.860
It is four days since they left the forest.

01:38:53.660 --> 01:38:56.260
They've been pushed north into less familiar terrain,

01:38:57.900 --> 01:38:58.880
tired from traveling.

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:02.320
They noticed the Dinatherium too late.

01:39:30.470 --> 01:39:39.020
This male is coming into must. His body is pumped full of testosterone ready for mating.

01:39:39.840 --> 01:39:42.500
Unfortunately, this makes him extremely aggressive.

01:39:44.250 --> 01:39:47.050
In the chaos, a youngster has become separated from the group.

01:39:47.640 --> 01:39:52.500
And now there is 14 tons of angry dinotherium between him and his mother.

01:40:02.110 --> 01:40:07.140
The youngster is Babbel's. His desperate calls to her are making her blind to danger.

01:40:13.380 --> 01:40:17.620
Babbel tries to reach the youngster, but she is soon running for her life instead.

01:40:30.030 --> 01:40:34.880
At least Babel distracts the Dinatherian, but then the youngster does the worst thing possible.

01:40:35.640 --> 01:40:37.100
He continues to call out.

01:41:19.450 --> 01:41:22.400
They very nearly lost another member of the group.

01:41:34.120 --> 01:41:38.810
A few days later, they have moved on and found a place that appears to have everything they need.

01:41:39.370 --> 01:41:43.740
There are trees to hide in, water to drink, and more fruit than they can eat.

01:41:51.000 --> 01:41:54.000
There don't appear to be any other Australopithecus around.

01:41:59.060 --> 01:41:59.980
They only have to share.

01:42:00.000 --> 01:42:06.400
They share the fruit with the Ancylotherium, and they can be scared off if you know how.

01:42:15.870 --> 01:42:20.250
Hercules has learned to use noise to appear more dangerous than he really is.

01:42:34.730 --> 01:42:37.430
The groups soon start to settle into their new surroundings.

01:42:40.810 --> 01:42:44.070
The first time in a few days, the youngest members of the group are playing again.

01:42:45.870 --> 01:42:47.710
But some things plainly haven't changed.

01:42:48.340 --> 01:42:49.880
Blue is still on the edge of things.

01:42:50.890 --> 01:42:55.040
Even though the other juveniles are younger than Blue, they ignore him.

01:43:03.840 --> 01:43:07.150
With darkness round the corner, it's time to prepare for evening.

01:43:08.360 --> 01:43:11.100
Australopithecus sleep in the trees because it is safer

01:43:11.100 --> 01:43:15.380
and make a new nest every night by folding down a bed of branches.

01:43:18.140 --> 01:43:21.780
Primates were once all nocturnal creatures, but not anymore.

01:43:22.640 --> 01:43:24.800
Some have evolved great colour vision,

01:43:25.130 --> 01:43:27.730
allowing them to pick out succulent leaves and fruit.

01:43:28.250 --> 01:43:29.970
But this has come at the expense of...

01:43:30.000 --> 01:43:33.600
their night vision, which is now poor compared to most animals.

01:43:34.140 --> 01:43:36.880
So for these apes, and in the future humans,

01:43:37.440 --> 01:43:39.460
the night is a time to sleep.

01:43:41.780 --> 01:43:43.840
Sadly, the Australopithecus will soon

01:43:43.840 --> 01:43:47.570
find that this place is not the paradise it first appears.

01:44:00.670 --> 01:44:08.850
Black Eye has stolen an egg from an ostrich nest.

01:44:09.400 --> 01:44:14.240
Being able to carry things while on the move is another advantage of walking on two legs.

01:44:25.670 --> 01:44:28.910
Before she can break into the egg, Grace...

01:44:30.000 --> 01:44:30.820
steals it from her.

01:44:58.600 --> 01:44:59.980
Dinah Felis is a kid.

01:45:00.000 --> 01:45:05.710
killer of Australopithecus. These cats don't have the speed to bring down fast-running

01:45:05.710 --> 01:45:13.620
herbivores, so specialize in slower prey that they can kill easily and eat quickly. Later

01:45:13.620 --> 01:45:18.860
members of the human family will rise up the food chain to become top predators, but

01:45:18.860 --> 01:45:21.620
in this era, our relatives are prey.

01:45:40.240 --> 01:45:43.080
After the attack, the group are quiet and subdued.

01:45:43.900 --> 01:45:45.580
Black Eye's death has left a hole.

01:45:46.510 --> 01:45:48.050
They spend a lot of their time grooming

01:45:48.050 --> 01:45:51.780
to make new social bonds and strengthen old ones.

01:45:52.430 --> 01:45:53.970
It's their equivalent of talking.

01:45:56.140 --> 01:45:58.820
At last, Blue seems to be interacting with the group

01:45:58.820 --> 01:45:59.960
and is learning to...

01:46:00.000 --> 01:46:11.950
play the social game. He is grooming grey. The group's numbers have now at least been

01:46:11.950 --> 01:46:17.210
boosted by the arrival of a young female. While males stay within the same group their

01:46:17.210 --> 01:46:22.520
whole lives, female Australopithecus move to a new group when they are old enough to

01:46:22.520 --> 01:46:29.380
mate. This prevents inbreeding. It is a difficult transition for her.

01:46:30.000 --> 01:46:32.700
It will take her some time to be accepted by the females.

01:46:36.780 --> 01:46:39.140
The males, though, show more immediate interest.

01:46:39.790 --> 01:46:41.850
She has a new possibility for mating.

01:46:45.190 --> 01:46:48.230
Normally, only the dominant male gets access to the females.

01:46:48.620 --> 01:46:51.760
But Hercules looks like he is about to try his luck.

01:47:04.100 --> 01:47:09.920
Unlike just about every other mammal on the planet, Australopithecus mate face-to-face.

01:47:10.570 --> 01:47:15.550
Walking upright has changed the orientation of the pelvis, and as a result, of the female

01:47:15.550 --> 01:47:16.770
sexual organs too.

01:47:22.680 --> 01:47:25.950
Gray has just noticed, and isn't having any of it.

01:47:32.740 --> 01:47:36.000
Blue's one attempt at making friends has been scuppered.

01:47:48.240 --> 01:47:54.460
When there is no fruit in season, the group's diet becomes more down-to-earth as they dig

01:47:54.460 --> 01:47:55.680
for roots and tubers.

01:47:56.680 --> 01:47:59.240
Australopithecus are masters of the varied diet.

01:48:00.220 --> 01:48:03.060
They have a number of adaptations for feeding themselves.

01:48:03.950 --> 01:48:06.850
Hands that have become nimble and dexterous,

01:48:07.010 --> 01:48:09.190
since they are no longer used for walking on.

01:48:11.080 --> 01:48:14.800
Thick enamel on their teeth to help them cope with tough or dirty food.

01:48:15.620 --> 01:48:16.360
And ingenuity.

01:48:17.310 --> 01:48:20.110
Australopithecus can use basic tools like sticks to dig.

01:48:23.290 --> 01:48:24.570
They are not born with this ability.

01:48:24.910 --> 01:48:27.110
They must learn it by imitating their elders.

01:48:30.000 --> 01:48:33.540
In Lou's case, his lessons were far from over when his mother died.

01:48:33.820 --> 01:48:38.160
He is having to learn fast because no one else is going to feed him.

01:48:49.380 --> 01:48:55.140
There is another very important element of their diet, meat.

01:48:56.260 --> 01:48:59.960
Out on the savannah, carrion is quick to attract scavengers.

01:49:00.000 --> 01:49:11.010
often the Australopithecus are among them but they have to be fast to beat the

01:49:11.010 --> 01:49:29.750
vultures no time at all they are all over the zebra carcass once again Hercules

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:34.460
tries out his scare tactics to great effect.

01:49:34.880 --> 01:49:49.120
There is no clearer display of the group's hierarchy

01:49:49.120 --> 01:49:50.980
than the order they get food.

01:49:52.910 --> 01:49:54.670
Gray is used to getting the first bite,

01:49:55.140 --> 01:49:57.800
but he finds Hercules is already getting stuck in.

01:50:01.820 --> 01:50:02.140
Woo!

01:50:09.060 --> 01:50:09.080
Woo!

01:50:09.400 --> 01:50:09.800
Woo!

01:50:09.940 --> 01:50:10.580
Woo!

01:50:33.450 --> 01:50:44.360
it is nothing short of a coup hercules returns to the carcass as top male at first the group are

01:50:44.360 --> 01:50:51.580
unsure how to react to the change of leadership gray meanwhile nurses his pride in the background

01:50:51.580 --> 01:50:59.200
meat is only a small part of the australopithecus diet but

01:51:00.000 --> 01:51:03.760
It will become more important in the future for more human-like apes.

01:51:07.100 --> 01:51:11.300
An increase in meat-eating will go hand-in-hand with an increase in brain size.

01:51:11.800 --> 01:51:14.580
Meat contains nutrients vital for big brains.

01:51:16.350 --> 01:51:19.150
Intelligent apes will develop special tools to get meat,

01:51:19.490 --> 01:51:23.810
until eventually they'll make weapons and won't be scavengers but predators.

01:51:35.080 --> 01:51:38.280
Hercules' takeover has an immediate positive effect.

01:51:39.100 --> 01:51:41.940
There is less internal conflict than there was before.

01:51:44.550 --> 01:51:46.970
But the group's biggest problem hasn't gone away.

01:51:48.580 --> 01:51:51.640
It is only a matter of time before the Dinophilus comes back.

01:52:35.020 --> 01:52:39.300
Blue is in dire trouble, unable to get to the tree in time.

01:52:39.930 --> 01:52:41.910
He is now an obvious target for the cat.

01:52:47.500 --> 01:52:50.600
But suddenly the Australopithecus do something extraordinary.

01:52:51.570 --> 01:52:56.450
The whole group spontaneously unites and tries to turn the tables on the hunter.

01:53:41.100 --> 01:53:43.670
For Blue, this is an important moment.

01:53:46.130 --> 01:53:51.450
Not only has he survived a deadly situation, something more profound has happened.

01:53:53.490 --> 01:53:57.930
He may be the lowest ranking member of this group, but he is now at least valued enough

01:53:57.930 --> 01:53:59.740
to spur them into action.

01:54:00.540 --> 01:54:01.400
It's a start.

01:54:08.640 --> 01:54:10.720
Apes have a long road to travel yet.

01:54:11.480 --> 01:54:13.120
Outwardly, they are almost human,

01:54:13.560 --> 01:54:16.260
but their brains are no bigger than a chimpanzee's.

01:54:16.940 --> 01:54:19.140
It will be at least another two million years

01:54:19.140 --> 01:54:22.470
before any ape has a decent conversation.

01:54:27.160 --> 01:54:29.980
Next time, The Lost Continent of S-

01:54:30.000 --> 01:54:35.190
South America, a world of strange exotic giants and a legendary killer.

01:54:36.050 --> 01:54:39.540
Smilodon, the largest of all the saber-toothed cats.

01:55:53.900 --> 01:55:59.760
For 40 million years, the plains of South America have been ruled by an awesome group.

01:56:00.120 --> 01:56:26.250
predators, aptly named terror birds, recently all this has changed, a new breed of killer

01:56:26.250 --> 01:56:29.960
has arrived, one fierce enough to terrorize the terror.

01:56:30.000 --> 01:56:52.860
birds. Smilodon, the fabled saber-tooth, the most powerful big cat of all time.

01:57:07.110 --> 01:57:10.250
There is no such thing as a saber-toothed tiger.

01:57:11.010 --> 01:57:13.850
The correct name for these beasts is saber-toothed cats,

01:57:14.030 --> 01:57:16.010
and there have been many different species.

01:57:16.640 --> 01:57:19.700
The largest, at over 300 kilograms, is this one,

01:57:20.370 --> 01:57:22.560
Smilodon, which means knife-tooth.

01:57:23.130 --> 01:57:24.550
Originally from North America,

01:57:24.710 --> 01:57:27.170
they came to South America two million years ago.

01:57:27.790 --> 01:57:29.970
The balance of life here changed as they took...

01:57:30.000 --> 01:57:31.640
took over the role of top predators.

01:57:32.820 --> 01:57:34.700
They live in tightly knit clans,

01:57:34.720 --> 01:57:37.420
and this is the story of three dramatic months

01:57:37.420 --> 01:57:39.200
in the life of one particular group

01:57:39.200 --> 01:57:42.580
led by a seven-year-old male called Half Tooth.

01:57:44.710 --> 01:57:46.910
Right from the beginning, Half Tooth has problems.

01:57:47.340 --> 01:57:49.640
Recently, a pair of rival males have appeared.

01:57:55.060 --> 01:57:56.840
They want Half Tooth's crown.

01:58:00.220 --> 01:58:16.830
Half-Tooth's territory spans several hundred square kilometers of grassland on one of the strangest continents on Earth.

01:58:17.590 --> 01:58:23.360
Since splitting from Antarctica 30 million years ago, South America has been isolated from the rest of the world,

01:58:23.630 --> 01:58:26.250
and as a result, its animals are unique.

01:58:27.020 --> 01:58:29.960
Oddities thrive here, like Didacurus, a spider.

01:58:30.000 --> 01:58:37.900
Psyche-tailed relative of the armadillo the size of a car even the large herds of herbivores are exotic

01:58:37.900 --> 01:58:45.410
These Macroquenia are unrelated to any modern mammal and then of course there are the native predators

01:58:45.410 --> 01:58:52.720
The flightless terror birds these are three meters tall and called forrest rachos

01:58:52.720 --> 01:58:59.980
But the isolation of these animals came to an end when the movement of the South American continent caused it to call

01:59:00.000 --> 01:59:06.230
with North America. Across a land bridge came new groups of mammals to colonize

01:59:06.230 --> 01:59:15.200
this exotic world. Life here would never be the same again. Cats were among those

01:59:15.200 --> 01:59:20.960
colonists. No cat had ever set foot in South America before, but now sabre-tooths

01:59:20.960 --> 01:59:28.890
rule the grasslands. Half-tooth took over this clan three years ago, breaking one

01:59:28.890 --> 01:59:29.970
sabre in the process.

01:59:30.000 --> 01:59:34.550
The group is now made up of four adult females and their offspring.

01:59:36.660 --> 01:59:38.460
All the youngsters are half-tooths.

01:59:42.710 --> 01:59:46.950
During his reign, half-tooth has seen off many challenges from rival males,

01:59:49.820 --> 01:59:51.880
but none as serious as this one.

01:59:52.920 --> 01:59:56.120
Two rival males have wandered deep into half-tooth's territory.

01:59:57.160 --> 01:59:59.180
As usual, he is quick to respond.

02:00:01.820 --> 02:00:04.850
But these are brothers, and they are working together.

02:00:08.140 --> 02:00:12.020
Even by Smilodon standards, Half-Tooth is a very large male,

02:00:12.670 --> 02:00:15.390
but he has never before faced two opponents at once.

02:00:15.920 --> 02:00:18.240
The odds this time are stacked against him.

02:00:27.350 --> 02:00:29.830
Females read the signals and start to retreat.

02:00:30.250 --> 02:00:33.070
Their only concern is to protect their offspring.

02:00:38.670 --> 02:00:42.230
These fights can be brutal, and Halftooth has the most to lose.

02:00:42.450 --> 02:00:46.210
Not only control of the clan, but possibly his life.

02:01:01.300 --> 02:01:13.370
No match for the brothers. Half-Tooth is quick to back down.

02:01:17.010 --> 02:01:21.280
He has at least escaped serious injury, but his problems are just beginning.

02:01:22.190 --> 02:01:25.570
Life for a lone male is a grim battle for survival.

02:01:34.380 --> 02:01:40.240
Half-Tooth hasn't had to feed himself for three years because it was the females in the clan that did the hunting.

02:01:41.530 --> 02:01:44.870
Outcast from the rich grasslands now controlled by the brothers,

02:01:45.190 --> 02:01:51.350
he heads into the scrub forest on the edges of his old territory where he must learn to scrape out a living.

02:01:53.720 --> 02:01:56.720
With many of the creatures here, he stands no chance.

02:01:57.740 --> 02:01:59.980
Dedecurus, for instance, are not only well-armed...

02:02:00.000 --> 02:02:01.220
They are huge.

02:02:02.320 --> 02:02:07.700
These are ancient relatives of the armadillo, yet they weigh almost 300 times as much.

02:02:13.410 --> 02:02:18.660
Their spiked tail clubs alone can weigh 40 kilos, as much as a cannonball.

02:02:19.730 --> 02:02:25.100
Although fearsome defensive weapons, the males mostly use their tails on each other to batter

02:02:25.100 --> 02:02:29.400
rivals into submission during hormone-fuelled jousts for a mate.

02:02:31.150 --> 02:02:34.390
The loser ends up with more than just his pride dented.

02:02:43.240 --> 02:02:45.830
In the clan, tensions are running high.

02:02:47.630 --> 02:02:49.710
The females will not accept the brothers

02:02:49.710 --> 02:02:59.790
while they still have young, defeating Half-Tooth.

02:03:00.220 --> 02:03:10.500
was only part of the battle if the brothers are to gain authority over the clan they'll have to

02:03:10.500 --> 02:03:22.560
kill the cubs meanwhile half tooth has pushed deeper into the scrub forest in search of food

02:03:22.560 --> 02:03:29.760
but if anything his task is getting more difficult the dense undergrowth harbors another colossus

02:03:30.480 --> 02:03:40.060
a sloth. But this is no ordinary sloth. This is megatherium, a giant ground sloth.

02:03:41.550 --> 02:03:47.470
It has huge claws, is six meters from nose to tail, and weighs more than an elephant,

02:03:48.350 --> 02:03:52.630
a very different beast from its distant relatives, the gentle tree sloths.

02:03:54.830 --> 02:03:59.920
half-tooth moves cautiously, the sloth all

02:04:00.000 --> 02:04:07.310
boasts impressive defenses. Small bones beneath his skin act like chain mail.

02:04:07.920 --> 02:04:15.280
Adults are virtually indestructible. Half tooth wisely gives them a wide

02:04:15.280 --> 02:04:29.870
birth. Weeks pass and the wets

02:04:30.000 --> 02:04:31.300
season is in full swing.

02:04:35.220 --> 02:04:36.840
The females have done their best,

02:04:37.310 --> 02:04:39.610
but only two of half-toothed cubs survive,

02:04:40.240 --> 02:04:42.260
and the brothers are a constant danger.

02:04:45.930 --> 02:04:49.170
They spend much of their time patrolling their new territory,

02:04:49.850 --> 02:04:51.570
spraying the boundaries with scent

02:04:51.570 --> 02:04:53.670
as a warning to other males.

02:05:02.110 --> 02:05:07.200
They will not attack the cubs while the mother is present, but she must abandon them to go hunting.

02:05:07.990 --> 02:05:14.060
This is when they are most at risk, and so this mother protects them by hiding them in the dense undergrowth.

02:05:20.200 --> 02:05:25.700
While the females are absent, the brothers are spotted dangerously close to the hiding place.

02:05:30.000 --> 02:05:31.500
Lives hang by a thread.

02:05:33.750 --> 02:05:36.690
Now their only hope is to remain motionless.

02:05:58.960 --> 02:05:59.840
Out on the plains.

02:06:00.000 --> 02:06:06.560
there are large herds of Macroquenia. Unique to South America, this species is the last of their

02:06:06.560 --> 02:06:29.380
kind. They are prime targets for Smilodon. Macroquenia are fast, wary animals, and getting

02:06:29.380 --> 02:06:29.980
close enough to...

02:06:30.000 --> 02:06:32.290
to kill one takes endless patience.

02:06:32.530 --> 02:06:36.070
The females hunt together as a pack,

02:06:36.590 --> 02:06:38.290
inching forward to set the trap.

02:06:39.340 --> 02:06:41.980
They have been closing in on this herd for two hours now.

02:06:43.020 --> 02:06:46.020
Gradually they fan out with the lead female moving wide,

02:06:46.460 --> 02:06:48.200
trying to encircle their prey.

02:06:50.400 --> 02:06:51.880
Smilodon are powerful killers

02:06:51.880 --> 02:06:54.000
and able to accelerate explosively,

02:06:54.560 --> 02:06:56.460
but they are not built for long chases.

02:06:57.400 --> 02:06:59.980
Unless they get close, they don't stand a chance.

02:07:00.000 --> 02:07:24.480
One false move and they will have to start all over again.

02:07:44.910 --> 02:07:47.530
female drives the herd towards the other females.

02:08:24.990 --> 02:08:27.670
chosen their target, the cats close in.

02:08:40.620 --> 02:08:47.080
Macroquenia have outstanding manoeuvrability, the victim jinx, completely wrong-footing her pursuer.

02:08:49.660 --> 02:08:54.470
But that's why Smilodon hunt in groups. A second cat brings her down.

02:09:11.200 --> 02:09:14.380
Surprisingly, as the cats fight to restrain their prey,

02:09:14.870 --> 02:09:18.740
they do not use their infamous teeth in case they break them.

02:09:23.320 --> 02:09:26.500
First, they work together to pin down the struggling animal

02:09:26.650 --> 02:09:28.230
under the weight of their bodies.

02:09:30.800 --> 02:09:38.100
Only when the prey is totally subdued are the famous sabres drawn.

02:09:43.570 --> 02:09:46.750
They are extraordinarily precise and accurate tools for killing.

02:09:47.400 --> 02:09:51.360
Their teeth simultaneously sever the blood supply and strangle the windpipe.

02:09:51.890 --> 02:09:53.730
One bite and the prey is dead.

02:09:54.490 --> 02:09:56.790
But these are the original double-edged swords.

02:09:57.250 --> 02:09:59.970
The lethal 20-centimeter sabres are actually...

02:10:00.000 --> 02:10:06.120
quite fragile. They cannot bite into bone, and so Smilodon can only eat the

02:10:06.120 --> 02:10:10.760
fleshier parts of a carcass. This is very wasteful, something that hasn't gone

02:10:10.760 --> 02:10:24.780
unnoticed by other carnivores on the grasslands. The terror birds are supreme

02:10:24.780 --> 02:10:29.180
opportunists. They may have been replaced as top predators, but they have

02:10:29.180 --> 02:10:29.860
quickly found

02:10:30.000 --> 02:10:50.280
new ways of surviving thanks to the cats eventually the Smilodon have eaten as

02:10:50.280 --> 02:10:54.380
much as their sabers will allow them they leave the kill for the birds to

02:10:54.380 --> 02:10:59.770
pick over South America is not the only place

02:11:00.000 --> 02:11:05.510
birds are doing well. Once the land bridge opened to North America, they were one of the few creatures

02:11:05.510 --> 02:11:11.310
to spread north successfully. While these terror birds eat Smilodon's leftovers, their cousins

02:11:11.310 --> 02:11:21.340
are running riot in Texas and Florida. They only died out just before modern humans appeared.

02:11:28.230 --> 02:11:29.980
After the hunt, the mother returned.

02:11:30.000 --> 02:11:33.420
to where she hid the cubs, as she has done several times

02:11:33.420 --> 02:11:34.440
over the last weeks.

02:11:38.380 --> 02:11:42.250
She calls to them, but her calls go unanswered.

02:11:58.230 --> 02:11:59.970
A half-tooth is soon spotted near me.

02:12:00.000 --> 02:12:04.040
roaming on the fringes of a territory that used to be his own.

02:12:08.540 --> 02:12:12.940
But where he once sprayed he can clearly smell the scent of the brothers.

02:12:17.360 --> 02:12:19.780
That is not the only signal they have left.

02:12:31.820 --> 02:12:35.810
With the death of his cubs, his last tie with the group has been severed.

02:12:36.550 --> 02:12:38.370
The takeover is complete.

02:12:54.580 --> 02:12:57.080
Sightings of half-tooth have started to become rare,

02:12:57.530 --> 02:12:59.970
and it is clear from his physical condition he is...

02:13:00.000 --> 02:13:05.980
not thriving in the scrub forest. It is dawn and half-tooth is exploiting his

02:13:05.980 --> 02:13:13.330
sensitive vision to hunt in the low light. Male Macrochinia live separately

02:13:13.330 --> 02:13:18.010
from the herds and often wander into the scrub forest to browse using their

02:13:18.010 --> 02:13:29.480
long noses to strip fresh leaves. Half-tooth waits.

02:13:30.060 --> 02:13:32.880
and allows the Macrokenia to wander ever closer.

02:13:33.840 --> 02:13:35.780
There must be no mistakes this time.

02:13:44.670 --> 02:13:47.650
But a giant ground sloth is about to ruin everything.

02:13:48.280 --> 02:13:49.960
As he crashes through the undergrowth,

02:13:50.420 --> 02:13:52.460
Half-Tooth's quarry starts moving away.

02:13:54.910 --> 02:13:56.030
It's now or never.

02:14:00.000 --> 02:14:11.920
Half-tooth is too weak to follow.

02:14:13.110 --> 02:14:18.290
The longer he goes without a kill, the smaller his chance of making one.

02:14:28.290 --> 02:14:29.290
Increasingly desperate.

02:14:30.000 --> 02:14:33.060
He is seen still patrolling the same area later that morning.

02:14:41.960 --> 02:14:45.700
He spots a macrockinia mother and young close to the forest edge.

02:14:46.580 --> 02:14:50.860
They have wandered away from the main herd, maybe this time.

02:15:00.380 --> 02:15:11.570
With a lightning strike of its massive beak and claws,

02:15:11.870 --> 02:15:15.030
a terror bird comes from nowhere to kill the youngster.

02:15:23.060 --> 02:15:25.020
But all is not lost for half-tooth.

02:15:26.640 --> 02:15:27.860
He seizes his chance.

02:15:43.680 --> 02:15:48.440
Scavenging is now his best hope, but after finishing this kill,

02:15:49.350 --> 02:15:51.710
half-tooth disappeared from this scrub forest.

02:16:02.200 --> 02:16:06.160
Not far away, the brothers have completely usurped Half-Tooth.

02:16:07.080 --> 02:16:10.790
With the last of the cubs dead, the clan has now accepted them.

02:16:16.490 --> 02:16:20.550
The remaining adolescents are tolerated by the brothers because they are female

02:16:20.550 --> 02:16:22.870
and will soon be old enough to mate.

02:16:25.400 --> 02:16:29.340
And with no cubs to suckle, the older females come into heat again.

02:16:30.250 --> 02:16:33.510
Killing half-toothed cubs was not just a pointless act of violence.

02:16:34.130 --> 02:16:38.890
With the cubs out of the way, the clan can now concentrate on raising the brothers' offspring

02:16:38.890 --> 02:16:42.010
and passing their genes on to the next generation.

02:16:51.180 --> 02:16:53.920
All in all, the clan is returning to normal.

02:16:55.510 --> 02:16:58.030
The young females practice their hunting skills.

02:17:02.430 --> 02:17:06.430
although the Didacurus they have picked on has little time for their games.

02:17:06.990 --> 02:17:24.640
The rainy season has come and gone and half-tooth has not been seen for weeks.

02:17:25.550 --> 02:17:29.890
His old clan, however, is very much in evidence. They have been making a kill

02:17:30.000 --> 02:17:33.790
every few days, and today they're once again feeding well.

02:17:37.940 --> 02:17:40.800
As usual, the terror birds are not far away,

02:17:41.920 --> 02:17:44.200
but on this occasion there is another scavenger,

02:17:44.750 --> 02:17:45.670
a megatherium.

02:17:46.180 --> 02:17:49.020
They might be herbivores, but they will occasionally go

02:17:49.020 --> 02:17:50.940
for carrion to supplement their diet,

02:17:50.980 --> 02:17:54.220
and when they do, nothing gets in their way.

02:18:04.190 --> 02:18:13.250
One of the brothers has been killed.

02:18:14.070 --> 02:18:17.270
The rest of the Smilodon quickly abandon the kill to the giant.

02:18:22.900 --> 02:18:27.140
In the blink of an eye, the megatherium has changed everything for the clan.

02:18:29.110 --> 02:18:29.970
Now there's only one.

02:18:30.000 --> 02:18:46.060
one brother left. Within days, Half-Tooth suddenly reappears. He must have been closely monitoring

02:18:46.060 --> 02:18:52.810
his old territory. So sensitive is the sense of smell in cats that he can tell the identity

02:18:52.810 --> 02:18:58.140
and even the health of a male who has left his scent. In this case, the marks are easy

02:18:58.140 --> 02:18:58.800
to interpret.

02:19:00.000 --> 02:19:02.840
Once two males, now there is only one.

02:19:12.700 --> 02:19:15.160
Within the clan, things are going badly

02:19:15.160 --> 02:19:16.440
for the remaining male.

02:19:17.740 --> 02:19:19.740
He was not the dominant of the two brothers,

02:19:20.330 --> 02:19:23.410
and the females appear to have become unsettled again.

02:19:32.010 --> 02:19:35.050
But it is not the females that the lone brother is worried about.

02:19:35.870 --> 02:19:39.910
It is only a matter of time before other males come looking for trouble.

02:19:53.010 --> 02:19:58.760
He continues to patrol his territory, but a challenge will come sooner than the brother expects.

02:20:04.190 --> 02:20:11.100
Half-Tooth is back. Encouraged by the signals, he is wasting no time in trying to reclaim the clan.

02:20:14.670 --> 02:20:16.670
A fresh scent trail leads him on.

02:20:40.170 --> 02:20:45.800
Three months after losing to the brothers, Half Tooth is once again about to fight for this territory.

02:20:55.870 --> 02:20:57.970
The odds this time have evened up.

02:21:30.440 --> 02:21:34.340
The fight is brief and brutal, but Half-Tooth triumphs.

02:21:35.060 --> 02:21:47.540
Brother is defeated and fatally injured.

02:21:54.000 --> 02:21:57.450
Unlike Half-Tooth, this cat will get no second chance.

02:22:04.760 --> 02:22:07.820
The short, violent reign of the brothers is over.

02:22:27.180 --> 02:22:29.980
A year later, finds Half-Tooth surrounded by...

02:22:30.000 --> 02:22:31.760
by new arrivals of his own.

02:22:32.580 --> 02:22:34.960
He is king of the grasslands again.

02:22:40.410 --> 02:22:42.510
Smilodon were very successful predators

02:22:42.510 --> 02:22:46.720
and survived up until as recently as 10,000 years ago.

02:22:48.470 --> 02:22:51.610
But eventually the world around them changed too much.

02:22:52.020 --> 02:22:54.160
The climate became cooler and drier.

02:22:54.640 --> 02:22:57.720
The larger prey they specialized in killing disappeared.

02:22:58.460 --> 02:22:59.840
The saber-toothed world.

02:23:00.730 --> 02:23:13.870
came to an end in our final episode things will get much much colder as we

02:23:13.870 --> 02:23:20.560
visit our ancestors in an ice age 30,000 years ago it was the toughest of times

02:23:20.560 --> 02:23:27.780
and gave rise to one of the most awesome mammals of all the mammoths

02:24:30.000 --> 02:24:45.130
For the last 50 million years, the world has been getting slowly colder.

02:24:46.600 --> 02:24:49.850
But something else has now helped tip it over the edge.

02:24:50.820 --> 02:24:54.400
A change in the Earth's orbit is taking it further from the sun.

02:24:55.280 --> 02:24:57.440
This has brought on an ice age.

02:24:58.430 --> 02:24:59.970
Conditions for all life becoming...

02:25:00.000 --> 02:25:03.080
Extremely hazardous, even for the mighty mammoths.

02:25:03.520 --> 02:25:14.000
The early autumn snowfall has concealed a barely frozen pond.

02:25:14.840 --> 02:25:17.580
This young female has fallen through the ice and is trapped.

02:25:21.120 --> 02:25:27.150
Such are the bonds between mammoths that the herd do not leave their stranded sister.

02:25:27.880 --> 02:25:29.800
They stay nearby, distressed.

02:25:30.220 --> 02:25:32.440
but powerless, except to comfort her.

02:25:49.290 --> 02:25:51.970
Before long, the scavengers have started to gather.

02:26:04.350 --> 02:26:08.820
Take days for the female to die, and the herd cannot afford to wait.

02:26:09.670 --> 02:26:11.710
Eventually they are forced to abandon her.

02:26:12.520 --> 02:26:17.450
They must keep moving to escape the coming Ice Age winter.

02:26:30.600 --> 02:26:35.770
For the few months of summer, there is little here to suggest that the world is in the middle of an ice age.

02:26:36.400 --> 02:26:40.660
An endless sea of grass and flowers supports a variety of animals.

02:26:41.390 --> 02:26:45.730
But just a few meters down, the ground is frozen solid all year round.

02:26:47.740 --> 02:26:52.520
There is now so much water frozen at the poles that sea levels have fallen dramatically.

02:26:53.190 --> 02:26:57.340
If it wasn't an ice age, this grassland would be the bottom of the North Sea.

02:26:58.150 --> 02:26:59.890
As it is, it's a magnet.

02:27:00.060 --> 02:27:02.520
for millions of grazing animals during the summer.

02:27:03.950 --> 02:27:07.060
The biggest grass eaters of them all are the mammoths.

02:27:09.320 --> 02:27:16.810
This program will be following one particular herd

02:27:16.810 --> 02:27:19.170
in their annual struggle against the elements.

02:27:19.990 --> 02:27:22.630
Like all mammoth herds, this one is mostly female

02:27:22.630 --> 02:27:24.830
since males leave at adolescence.

02:27:25.400 --> 02:27:28.470
There are five adult females, a 10-year-old male,

02:27:28.610 --> 02:27:29.970
and smallest of all,

02:27:30.700 --> 02:27:36.930
male calf just six months old. Born in the spring he has yet to experience how

02:27:36.930 --> 02:27:46.160
bad a winter can be. The eldest member is the matriarch. She is a veteran of some

02:27:46.160 --> 02:27:51.690
40 winters and it is her experience that ensures the survival of the herd when

02:27:51.690 --> 02:27:52.730
the going gets tough.

02:28:01.600 --> 02:28:05.360
Mammoths are just one of many herbivores to enjoy the summer bounty.

02:28:06.400 --> 02:28:09.320
They are all animals in their own way built for the cold.

02:28:10.190 --> 02:28:13.590
The mammoths and bison have their characteristic shaggy coats,

02:28:13.850 --> 02:28:17.010
while the saiga antelopes have another adaptation.

02:28:17.820 --> 02:28:29.610
Broad noses help them warm up the air before they breathe it with so much gain.

02:28:30.000 --> 02:28:32.280
It is not surprising there are also predators.

02:28:36.170 --> 02:28:38.150
Humans hunt on these plains in summer.

02:28:38.660 --> 02:28:41.400
They have no physical adaptation to cope with the cold,

02:28:41.600 --> 02:28:45.780
but instead wear animal hides and weave cloth to protect themselves.

02:28:52.120 --> 02:28:54.460
These predators specialize in small prey

02:28:54.460 --> 02:28:58.180
and seldom hunt anything as big and dangerous as the mammoths.

02:28:58.940 --> 02:28:59.980
But mammoths are none...

02:29:00.000 --> 02:29:07.260
very important to them. With so little wood on parts of the plain, they collect

02:29:07.260 --> 02:29:14.540
the tusks of dead animals to build huts and even use their bones for fuel. These

02:29:14.540 --> 02:29:19.260
most inventive of creatures are capable of making the most of every resource

02:29:19.260 --> 02:29:29.970
available. Of all the summer visitors, the most numerous are the flying

02:29:30.000 --> 02:29:37.830
which are a constant source of irritation for the mammoths the humans

02:29:37.830 --> 02:29:41.630
however have learned to do something about them they grind up a paste made

02:29:41.630 --> 02:29:51.000
out of a mineral called ochre which helps keep the insects at bay come the

02:29:51.000 --> 02:29:55.060
autumn this plane will become a freezing hellhole so the mammoths make

02:29:55.060 --> 02:29:59.650
the most of the summer while they can they eat up to 180

02:30:00.000 --> 02:30:05.250
kilograms of grass a day, while the males mate with as many females as possible.

02:30:39.690 --> 02:30:41.870
It's early autumn and the big freeze has begun.

02:30:42.680 --> 02:30:44.900
The mammoths are still on the northern plains.

02:30:46.990 --> 02:30:49.950
These creatures evolved from hairless elephants in Africa

02:30:49.950 --> 02:30:52.950
that have become living fortresses against the cold.

02:30:53.800 --> 02:30:55.560
Under their skin, they have a layer of fat

02:30:55.560 --> 02:30:57.560
10 centimeters thick for insulation.

02:30:58.340 --> 02:30:59.980
Also, they have small ears and...

02:31:00.000 --> 02:31:06.070
short tails to prevent them losing too much heat. On top of all this they have

02:31:06.070 --> 02:31:10.740
their characteristic carpet of hair which has now grown to a meter in length as

02:31:10.760 --> 02:31:26.600
their bodies respond to the drop in temperatures. As the weeks pass the true

02:31:26.600 --> 02:31:28.640
savagery of the climate begins to bite.

02:31:31.720 --> 02:31:36.880
The humans have already left the plains to seek shelter and milder weather further south.

02:31:44.870 --> 02:31:46.650
Soon the mammoths too will go.

02:31:47.340 --> 02:31:53.520
With little grass available and no protection from a wind chill that will average minus 50 degrees centigrade,

02:31:54.360 --> 02:31:56.200
even they have to move south.

02:32:00.830 --> 02:32:03.110
This ice age is in fact only one of many.

02:32:03.830 --> 02:32:09.590
Two and a half million years ago, the Earth's climate started on a roller coaster of warmer and colder periods.

02:32:10.110 --> 02:32:15.570
There have been almost 50 ice ages so far, but this is the coldest yet.

02:32:20.130 --> 02:32:25.860
Matriarch decides it is time to head off towards the less exposed valleys of the Alps.

02:32:32.430 --> 02:32:36.630
It is a 400-kilometer journey that they are driven to every year.

02:33:04.350 --> 02:33:08.290
For the megaloceros, autumn is not a time to travel, it is a time to rut.

02:33:08.880 --> 02:33:11.600
These males are fighting for the harem of females nearby.

02:33:12.260 --> 02:33:13.380
Winner takes all.

02:33:15.820 --> 02:33:20.720
Megaloceros are a giant form of deer, and the males sport a most impressive set of antlers.

02:33:22.240 --> 02:33:24.120
Each one is as long as a person.

02:33:25.660 --> 02:33:27.640
It's a wonder they can even hold their heads up.

02:33:32.110 --> 02:33:35.820
Exhausted from fighting, neither has noticed they are in a trap.

02:33:46.720 --> 02:33:49.040
Humans are predators unlike any before.

02:33:50.770 --> 02:33:52.830
They use not strength, but strategy,

02:33:53.540 --> 02:33:57.180
pushing the megaloceros into the trees where their antlers prevent escape.

02:34:00.000 --> 02:34:02.060
Only one of them has the energy to break out.

02:34:40.750 --> 02:34:45.430
The humans will have to take what they want as quickly as possible before Ice Age scavengers

02:34:45.430 --> 02:34:50.350
like wolves, lions and hyenas make this a dangerous place to be.

02:35:09.120 --> 02:35:13.770
Halfway through their journey and the mammoths are progressing well, but for one member of

02:35:13.770 --> 02:35:15.800
the herd it is a constant struggle.

02:35:16.630 --> 02:35:17.190
The youngest.

02:35:17.860 --> 02:35:19.200
This is his first winter.

02:35:20.080 --> 02:35:22.120
Many yearlings never see their second.

02:35:30.570 --> 02:35:32.930
They soon catch up with other migrating animals.

02:35:33.440 --> 02:35:35.400
Humans are also heading for the Alps,

02:35:35.700 --> 02:35:39.140
where the extensive cave networks make perfect winter retreats.

02:35:39.860 --> 02:35:41.880
The matriarch keeps a wary eye on them,

02:35:42.060 --> 02:35:45.130
and the humans know better than to get too close.

02:36:07.420 --> 02:36:10.760
The days pass and the calf is starting to get into trouble.

02:36:11.440 --> 02:36:13.620
He is having real difficulty keeping up.

02:36:16.430 --> 02:36:19.410
His mother stays with him, but the herd cannot wait

02:36:19.410 --> 02:36:21.720
and the pair are starting to fall behind.

02:36:31.530 --> 02:36:34.230
On this snowscape, a lion might seem out of place,

02:36:34.370 --> 02:36:36.460
but they are common in Europe at this time,

02:36:36.980 --> 02:36:42.470
and a baby mammoth in trouble is just the sort of thing to bring this cave lion out of its den.

02:36:47.810 --> 02:36:51.300
Defended by a full-grown adult, the baby is relatively safe,

02:36:51.750 --> 02:36:55.720
but the lion continues to stalk, hoping for the mother's guard to slip.

02:36:56.920 --> 02:36:59.200
The pair have now completely lost the herd.

02:37:11.480 --> 02:37:14.700
Across the landscape, other mammoths are making the same journey.

02:37:15.370 --> 02:37:17.940
This is an adult male, and he travels alone.

02:37:19.140 --> 02:37:22.170
There are lions in his way, feasting on a straggler.

02:37:22.570 --> 02:37:25.860
In this case, not a mammoth calf, but a human.

02:37:43.020 --> 02:37:44.720
Lions will have to let him pass.

02:37:45.570 --> 02:37:48.470
Nothing gets in the way of a mammoth migrating.

02:37:55.950 --> 02:37:59.970
Mammoths are sociable animals, and as the forests around them become...

02:38:00.030 --> 02:38:09.360
denser, so the small herds start to join up. Soon hundreds of mammoths are winding their

02:38:09.360 --> 02:38:11.940
way through the wintry lowlands of Europe.

02:38:31.240 --> 02:38:34.840
As the mammoths near the Alsace region, the terrain changes dramatically.

02:38:35.380 --> 02:38:37.860
Flat plains give way to hills and valleys.

02:38:38.180 --> 02:38:44.440
It is here that lives another species of human, the Neanderthals.

02:38:50.770 --> 02:38:54.370
They are much shorter than the other humans, and like other Ice Age animals,

02:38:54.590 --> 02:38:57.430
they have big noses to warm the air they breathe.

02:38:58.320 --> 02:38:59.980
Rather than migrate, they...

02:39:00.000 --> 02:39:04.730
They stay within the same valleys all year long, weathering out the worst of the winter

02:39:04.730 --> 02:39:06.130
in shallow caves.

02:39:07.500 --> 02:39:10.940
But with this recent ice age, their numbers have plummeted.

02:39:11.880 --> 02:39:13.880
This is now an endangered species.

02:39:14.160 --> 02:39:27.910
Another animal that doesn't travel south in winter is the woolly rhino.

02:39:29.280 --> 02:39:29.980
They are very...

02:39:30.580 --> 02:39:35.340
And this huge male has not even noticed the Neanderthal just 50 meters away.

02:39:41.670 --> 02:39:44.630
While the Neanderthal doesn't notice the rhino either.

02:39:52.750 --> 02:39:56.940
Woolly rhinos are easily startled and will charge at anything that surprises them.

02:39:57.840 --> 02:39:59.960
Even at this distance he won't see the Neanderthal.

02:40:00.090 --> 02:40:06.040
But his sense of smell is better than his sight the rhino has picked up the scent

02:40:39.692 --> 02:40:41.452
It is fortunate for the Neanderthal

02:40:41.804 --> 02:40:45.564
that his species is stronger and more heavily built than the other humans.

02:40:46.092 --> 02:40:47.852
Thick bones and a barrelled chest.

02:40:48.364 --> 02:40:50.964
help him deal with a very tough lifestyle.

02:41:21.500 --> 02:41:24.500
traveled almost 400 kilometers in two weeks.

02:41:24.764 --> 02:41:28.064
The herd at last arrives at the foothills of the Alps.

02:41:29.084 --> 02:41:29.984
Here in the shell.

02:41:30.000 --> 02:41:41.000
forests and valleys they will spend their winter. Here there is protection

02:41:41.000 --> 02:41:44.720
from the wind as well as food thanks to their highly

02:41:45.000 --> 02:41:50.100
dexterous trunks, these grassland animals can sustain themselves on trees like these

02:41:50.100 --> 02:41:54.580
spruce, when their usual food is buried under thick snow.

02:42:06.812 --> 02:42:07.512
A week later,

02:42:07.900 --> 02:42:10.140
and the matriarch hears a familiar call.

02:42:13.052 --> 02:42:14.392
the calf and his mother.

02:42:14.396 --> 02:42:16.996
have at last caught up with the rest of the herd.

02:42:43.080 --> 02:42:44.460
touching each other with their trunks,

02:42:45.000 --> 02:42:47.320
Members of the herd renew their bonds with the pair.

02:42:47.560 --> 02:42:49.990
The most hazardous part of the winter is over,

02:42:50.090 --> 02:42:52.110
and the calf has survived.

02:43:19.730 --> 02:43:21.730
In an ice age, spring comes late.

02:43:21.870 --> 02:43:25.670
It is May before things have warmed up enough for the snow to melt

02:43:25.670 --> 02:43:27.820
and a carpet of flowers to spring up.

02:43:30.480 --> 02:43:35.600
With the increase in temperature, the woolly rhinos have molted and are not as woolly as they were.

02:43:35.840 --> 02:43:44.270
The mammoths, too, have already shed their long winter coats to leave a lighter underwool only a few centimetres long.

02:43:49.480 --> 02:43:51.640
The calf has come through the winter in good health,

02:43:51.760 --> 02:43:55.920
and now he has been weaned, spends more time away from his mother.

02:43:56.060 --> 02:43:59.980
He is also no longer the only calf in the family.

02:44:00.000 --> 02:44:09.460
youngest. The herd's latest member arrived just a few weeks ago. It's a

02:44:09.460 --> 02:44:24.980
little female. The rhinos meanwhile are starting to get feisty again. These two

02:44:24.980 --> 02:44:28.500
males are fighting for territory but these confrontations aren't always

02:44:28.500 --> 02:44:29.890
violent. They scrape

02:44:30.000 --> 02:44:34.330
their horns on the ground until one of them backs down and leaves the area to

02:44:34.330 --> 02:44:51.360
the victor. But while winter may be over here the northern plains are still

02:44:51.360 --> 02:44:56.390
defrosting and so the herd will spend a couple of weeks in the valleys yet. It

02:44:56.390 --> 02:44:59.790
gives the newest arrival a bit more time to get some strength in her legs.

02:45:00.250 --> 02:45:01.650
before the journey back north.

02:45:11.760 --> 02:45:14.320
There is a downside to all this warmer weather.

02:45:15.280 --> 02:45:17.000
Insects are becoming a problem again.

02:45:19.000 --> 02:45:22.840
But where there's mud, there's a way.

02:45:33.480 --> 02:45:46.000
A good shower of mud can help prevent the insects biting or maybe mammoths just do this for the fun of it.

02:46:34.830 --> 02:46:41.220
Like autumn in reverse, the great mammoth caravans form once more as it's time to head north.

02:46:50.280 --> 02:46:54.490
Soon the mountains are behind them, but they are coming into dangerous territory.

02:47:05.760 --> 02:47:07.220
The Neanderthals are waiting.

02:47:08.300 --> 02:47:12.100
For them, the mammoths coming north is one of the most important times of the year.

02:47:12.870 --> 02:47:15.860
They are about to risk life and limb to hunt them.

02:47:18.560 --> 02:47:21.120
Take on a mammoth, they need every advantage they can get.

02:47:23.440 --> 02:47:26.730
They wait for dusk when the cliff will become less visible

02:47:26.730 --> 02:47:29.980
and they can make effective use of their sea.

02:47:30.000 --> 02:47:30.660
a secret weapon.

02:47:32.670 --> 02:47:32.970
Fire.

02:48:00.220 --> 02:48:00.740
Awesome.

02:49:07.420 --> 02:49:10.900
The Neanderthals descend to the bottom of the cliff to butcher their kills.

02:49:12.000 --> 02:49:13.520
One of the mammoths is still alive.

02:49:24.820 --> 02:49:29.980
The Neanderthals are supreme hunters, which is why they have lasted over 260,000 years.

02:49:30.000 --> 02:49:36.800
years, but they have a weakness. They do not adapt well to change. As a result of the harsher

02:49:36.800 --> 02:49:43.340
climate and the arrival of other humans, Neanderthals are fast disappearing. In another

02:49:43.340 --> 02:49:53.300
2,000 years, they'll be gone altogether. The herd eventually arrive back on the northern

02:49:53.300 --> 02:49:57.260
plains. Fortunately, both of the youngsters have survived the journey.

02:50:00.090 --> 02:50:02.710
The herd has nonetheless suffered a major loss.

02:50:03.440 --> 02:50:07.360
The matriarch is missing, killed, when the Neanderthals attacked.

02:50:10.850 --> 02:50:13.570
It is now her sister that leads the herd into the future.

02:50:15.400 --> 02:50:18.140
Mammoths will last another 24,000 years.

02:50:18.850 --> 02:50:21.630
They are superbly adapted for this cold world.

02:50:22.180 --> 02:50:27.130
But when it gets warm again, the grassy plains they depend on will disappear.

02:50:31.880 --> 02:50:34.340
The humans have a different future ahead of them.

02:50:35.340 --> 02:50:38.260
When change comes, they will be able to deal with it,

02:50:38.930 --> 02:50:40.110
because they can adapt,

02:50:40.900 --> 02:50:44.360
and they have the ability to shape the world around them.

02:50:46.980 --> 02:50:51.380
One day, they'll look back on all this.

02:51:13.690 --> 02:51:19.850
We have since built museums to celebrate the past and spent decades studying prehistoric lives.

02:51:20.010 --> 02:51:28.270
And if all this has taught us anything, it's this. No species lasts forever.
